1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,487
Chapter 45: Preterm Birth.

3
00:00:05,487 --> 00:00:29,487
Definition Of Preterm Birth Preterm Birth Rate Trends Preterm Newborn Morbidity Causes Of Preterm Birth Contributing Factors Diagnosis Preterm Birth Prevention Management Of Preterm Prelabor Rupture Of Membranes Management Of Preterm Labor With Intact Membranes References Preterm birth is a substantial global health issue with significant consequences to the newborn, family, and society.

4
00:00:29,487 --> 00:00:40,287
Preterm delivery affects nearly 15 million births worldwide, is the leading cause of death in children younger than 5 years, and remains a prominent issue in obstetrics .

5
00:00:40,287 --> 00:00:50,575
Although the burden of preterm birth is clear, defining the biology of human parturition and identifying strategies to reduce preterm birth rates remain elusive.

6
00:00:50,575 --> 00:00:53,200
Definition Of Preterm Birth.

7
00:00:53,200 --> 00:01:00,500
Preterm birth is delivery before 37 completed weeks, that is, before 366/7 weeks.

8
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,887
Subdivisions vary among organizations.

9
00:01:03,887 --> 00:01:15,550
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021b), births occurring between 34 and 36 completed weeks are considered late preterm.

10
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:25,837
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recognizes this late preterm definition but also labels births before 336/7 weeks as early preterm .

11
00:01:25,837 --> 00:01:41,700
Instead, the World Health Organization (2018) defines births before 28 completed weeks as extremely preterm, those from 28 to 32 weeks as very preterm, and from 32 to 37 weeks as moderate to late preterm.

12
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:52,512
These definitions lack a functional basis and should be distinguished from the concept of prematurity, which represents incomplete development of various organ systems at birth.

13
00:01:52,512 --> 00:01:59,187
For example, the lungs are particularly affected and may be susceptible to the respiratory distress syndrome .

14
00:01:59,187 --> 00:02:06,937
Similarly, neonates born before term can be small or large for gestational age but are still preterm by definition.

15
00:02:06,937 --> 00:02:20,812
Low birthweight refers to neonates weighing 1500 to 2500 g; very low birthweight describes those between 1000 and 1500 g; and extremely low birthweight refers to those <1000 g .

16
00:02:20,812 --> 00:02:23,312
Preterm Birth Rate Trends.

17
00:02:23,312 --> 00:02:33,025
In the United States, the preterm birth rate rose from 10.02 percent in 2018 to 10.23 percent for 2019.

18
00:02:33,025 --> 00:02:38,200
When interpreting these data, important factors and trends merit discussion.

19
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:47,100
First, during the past two decades, the percentage of preterm neonates fell from 2007 to 2014 but has since risen .

20
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:53,837
Some argue that the rate drop reflected changes in obstetrical dating criteria rather than true declines .

21
00:02:53,837 --> 00:03:05,400
Specifically, beginning in 2014, the National Center for Health Statistics transitioned to a new standard for estimating newborn gestational age for birth certificate completion .

22
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:14,637
The new measure, which is the obstetrical estimate of gestational age at delivery, replaced calculations based on the date of the last menstrual period.

23
00:03:14,637 --> 00:03:21,600
These measures differ and do not provide equivalent absolute numerical comparisons of preterm birth rates.

24
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:28,762
Thus, current national data are now not directly comparable to previously reported preterm birth rates.

25
00:03:28,762 --> 00:03:41,100
Although the percentage of neonates born early preterm rose slightly, the rise in the overall preterm rate from 2018 to 2019 was due primarily to the increase in late-preterm births.

26
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:46,175
Rates of these preterm births have gradually risen since 2013 .

27
00:03:46,175 --> 00:03:54,012
Notably, national data do not separate spontaneous preterm births from those that may be prompted for medical indication.

28
00:03:54,012 --> 00:04:00,600
One disturbing aspect of preterm birth rate trends in the United States is persistent disparities.

29
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:07,325
Rates of preterm birth among black women are markedly elevated above those for white and Hispanic women .

30
00:04:07,325 --> 00:04:16,675
Some investigators attribute this to differing socioeconomical circumstances (Collins, 2007; Leveno, 2009).

31
00:04:16,675 --> 00:04:27,700
The rates of preterm birth in the United States are also higher compared with those in other industrialized countries (Ananth, 2009; Delnord, 2017).

32
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:31,887
Internationally, preterm birth rates also are rising .

33
00:04:31,887 --> 00:04:37,575
Last, births in singleton pregnancies should be analyzed separately from all births.

34
00:04:37,575 --> 00:04:44,550
Namely, multifetal gestations have a shorter average gestational length and raise preterm birth rates .

35
00:04:44,550 --> 00:04:47,075
Preterm Newborn Morbidity

36
00:04:47,236 --> 00:04:49,136
And Mortality.

37
00:04:49,086 --> 00:05:00,973
In 2018 in the United States, 21,498 infants died in their first year of life, and 66 percent of infant deaths were among those born preterm.

38
00:05:00,973 --> 00:05:07,611
Gestational age at delivery and the risk of neonatal morbidity and mortality are inversely related.

39
00:05:07,611 --> 00:05:20,686
Namely, neonates born in the early-preterm period make up the smallest proportion of births, but these infants experience disproportionately higher rates of prematurity-related complications, including death .

40
00:05:20,686 --> 00:05:33,661
For context, the infant mortality rate in those born at less than 28 weeks’ gestation was 186 times higher than in those born at 37 to 41 weeks in the United States in 2018.

41
00:05:33,661 --> 00:05:36,186
Table 45-1.

42
00:05:36,186 --> 00:05:45,361
Infant Mortality Rates in the United States in 2018 The joint effect of gestational age and infant condition at birth also has been examined.

43
00:05:45,361 --> 00:05:54,036
Data from the Swedish Medical Birth Register correlated Apgar scores at 5 and 10 minutes in preterm neonates across gestational ages.

44
00:05:54,036 --> 00:05:59,223
The relative risk of death consistently increased with decreased Apgar scores .

45
00:05:59,223 --> 00:06:06,236
Low Apgar scores in preterm newborns may reflect biological immaturity rather than fetal depression.

46
00:06:06,236 --> 00:06:15,298
However, Apgar scores and changes in the score between 5 and 10 minutes were associated with neonatal mortality among preterm newborns.

47
00:06:15,298 --> 00:06:23,511
Preterm neonates who survive are at risk for a range of short- and longterm morbidities, largely due to organ system immaturity .

48
00:06:23,511 --> 00:06:29,586
That said, remarkable strides have been made in neonatal survival for those born preterm.

49
00:06:29,586 --> 00:06:43,723
In a study of more than 18,000 newborns weighing between 400 and 1500 g or aged between 22 and 32 weeks’ gestation, survival rates were analyzed as a function of both birthweight and gestational age .

50
00:06:43,723 --> 00:06:58,711
After achieving a birthweight ≥1000 g, a gestational age ≥28 weeks for females, or age ≥30 weeks for males, survival rates reach 95 percent.

51
00:06:58,711 --> 00:07:01,236
Table 45-2.

52
00:07:01,236 --> 00:07:07,623
Major Short- and Long-Term Problems in Very-LowBirthweight Infants # Threshold of Viability.

53
00:07:07,623 --> 00:07:15,186
Births once considered to be “abortuses” because the fetus weighed <500 g are now classified as live births.

54
00:07:15,186 --> 00:07:23,061
In the United States in 2019, 5189 live births weighed <500 g .

55
00:07:23,061 --> 00:07:28,836
Fortunately, perinatal and neonatal care has advanced tremendously for these births.

56
00:07:28,836 --> 00:07:38,298
As a result, the threshold of viability, which is the lower limit of fetal maturation compatible with extrauterine survival, has been reassessed.

57
00:07:38,298 --> 00:07:45,748
Currently, the threshold of viability lies between 200/7 and 256/7 weeks’ gestation.

58
00:07:45,748 --> 00:07:51,761
Neonates born in this periviable period are vulnerable because of their immature organ systems.

59
00:07:51,761 --> 00:07:59,036
Complications include brain injury and sepsis, both described in Chapter 34, (p. 618).

60
00:07:59,036 --> 00:08:06,923
To guide obstetrical decision-making and care of these fetuses, a national perinatal workshop convened in 2013 .

61
00:08:06,923 --> 00:08:13,811
The executive summary statement from this meeting served as the foundation for an Obstetric Care Consensus document .

62
00:08:13,811 --> 00:08:23,786
Periviable Neonatal Survival Delivery before 23 weeks’ gestation often results in death, and survival rates approximate 5 to 10 percent .

63
00:08:23,786 --> 00:08:27,436
Among survivors, morbidity is significant.

64
00:08:27,436 --> 00:08:35,711
Practices regarding active resuscitation vary between institutions and may explain the differing perinatal outcomes among these.

65
00:08:35,711 --> 00:08:45,736
Neonatal survival rates also vary among international comparisons, and rates at 24 weeks’ gestation range from 35 to 84 percent .

66
00:08:45,736 --> 00:08:48,273
Figure 45-1.

67
00:08:48,273 --> 00:08:53,423
Neonatal survival rates according to condition at birth and gestational age.

68
00:08:53,423 --> 00:09:13,273
Ishii (2013) data curve reflects liveborn survival rates; Stoll (2010) curve reflects liveborn survival rates; Rysavy (2015) curve reflects overall survival rates; Younge (2017) curve reflects survival and neurodevelopmental outcomes.

69
00:09:13,273 --> 00:09:24,111
A retrospective analysis of infants born at the University of Iowa from 2006 to 2015 at 22 to 25 weeks’ gestation underscores this issue .

70
00:09:24,111 --> 00:09:38,548
After excluding anomalous fetuses, death in the delivery room, and parental request for palliation, the neurodevelopmental outcomes were analyzed for 169 of 214 survivors at 18 to 22 months.

71
00:09:38,548 --> 00:09:45,461
Active management was defined as antenatal corticosteroids and resuscitation when desired by the parents.

72
00:09:45,461 --> 00:09:49,136
The survival rate was lower in neonates born at

73
00:09:49,300 --> 00:09:54,600
22 to 23 weeks’ than at 24 to 25 weeks’ gestation.

74
00:09:54,550 --> 00:10:03,587
However, mild or no neurodevelopmental impairment was reported in 64 percent of the surviving 22- to 23-week infants.

75
00:10:03,587 --> 00:10:10,100
An important caveat when examining perinatal outcomes, however, is ascertainment bias.

76
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:22,537
For example, the mean survival rate is 45 percent if the denominator is all live births compared with 72 percent if the denominator is only newborns admitted to neonatal intensive care .

77
00:10:22,537 --> 00:10:31,037
Another source of bias is use of multicenter datasets with considerable differences in obstetrical and early neonatal interventions .

78
00:10:31,037 --> 00:10:57,075
To evaluate contemporaneous outcomes of neonates born at 22 to 24 weeks, the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development (NICHD) Neonatal Research Network reported both survival and neurodevelopmental outcomes across consecutive birth-year epochs of 2000 to 2003, 2004 to 2007, and 2008 to 2011 in infants aged 18 to 22 months .

79
00:10:57,075 --> 00:11:07,187
The percentage of infants who survived rose significantly from 30 percent in 2000 to 2003 to 36 percent in 2008 to 2011.

80
00:11:07,187 --> 00:11:17,025
The percentage of infants who survived without neurodevelopmental impairment also significantly grew from 16 percent to 20 percent during the same time period.

81
00:11:17,025 --> 00:11:30,625
Although rates of survival without neurodevelopmental impairment increased over time for those born at 23 and 24 weeks, only 1 percent of infants born at 22 weeks survived without neurodevelopmental impairment .

82
00:11:30,625 --> 00:11:34,250
Somewhat similar results were published from Sweden.

83
00:11:34,250 --> 00:11:41,375
This report details a national population-based study of all neonates born at 22 to 26 weeks.

84
00:11:41,375 --> 00:11:49,612
Rates of survival to 1 year from 2004 to 2007 were compared with rates from 2014 to 2016.

85
00:11:49,612 --> 00:11:58,450
Survival rates at 1 year among live-born neonates at 22 to 26 weeks’ gestation significantly improved during the later period .

86
00:11:58,450 --> 00:12:01,050
Table 45-3.

87
00:12:01,050 --> 00:12:17,162
One-Year Survival Among Infants Delivered at 22–26 Weeks’ Gestational Age in Sweden Clinical Management The Periviable Birth Obstetric Care Consensus document also addresses management options based on the clinical characteristics of a given pregnancy.

88
00:12:17,162 --> 00:12:22,212
Nonmodifiable factors are fetal gender, weight, and plurality.

89
00:12:22,212 --> 00:12:34,250
Potentially modifiable antepartum and intrapartum factors include the location of delivery, intent to intervene by cesarean delivery, and administration of antenatal corticosteroids .

90
00:12:34,250 --> 00:12:40,262
Postnatal management addresses the initiation or withdrawal of intensive care after birth.

91
00:12:40,262 --> 00:12:42,887
Table 45-4.

92
00:12:42,887 --> 00:12:50,025
General Guidance for Threatened Periviable Birth Cesarean delivery at the threshold of viability is controversial.

93
00:12:50,025 --> 00:13:02,562
If the fetus-neonate is perceived to be too immature for aggressive support, cesarean delivery for common indications such as breech presentation or nonreassuring fetal heart rate patterns might be preempted.

94
00:13:02,562 --> 00:13:11,962
Moreover, observational studies have been inconsistent in demonstrating a benefit of cesarean delivery for the sole indication of periviability.

95
00:13:11,962 --> 00:13:22,962
Moreover, cesarean delivery may predispose the gravida to adverse intraoperative events and morbidity (Bertholdt, 2018; Blanc, 2019).

96
00:13:22,962 --> 00:13:36,187
In a study of 2906 singletons between 240/7 and 316/7 weeks eligible for attempted vaginal birth, 84 percent of cephalic presenting fetuses were delivered vaginally .

97
00:13:36,187 --> 00:13:42,687
Neonatal mortality rates did not differ compared with those associated with planned cesarean delivery.

98
00:13:42,687 --> 00:13:50,425
For breech presentations, however, relative risk for mortality was threefold higher with attempted vaginal delivery.

99
00:13:50,425 --> 00:14:00,587
In another study, Werner and colleagues (2013) analyzed 20,231 newborns delivered at 24 to 34 weeks.

100
00:14:00,587 --> 00:14:11,637
Cesarean delivery did not protect against poor outcomes such as neonatal death, intraventricular hemorrhage, seizures, respiratory distress, and subdural hemorrhage.

101
00:14:11,637 --> 00:14:22,750
From these findings, the Obstetric Care Consensus proposes that cesarean delivery be considered for fetal indications at 230/7 to 246/7 weeks.

102
00:14:22,750 --> 00:14:28,875
However, before 22 weeks, this route is reserved only for maternal indications.

103
00:14:28,875 --> 00:14:41,912
As emphasized by the authors of the Iowa report cited earlier, providing active management—such as antenatal corticosteroids—can be uncoupled from the decision of cesarean in the periviable period .

104
00:14:41,912 --> 00:14:43,337
That

105
00:14:43,492 --> 00:14:54,154
said, the frequency of cesarean in the 23rd week has increased in the United States following publication of the perinatal workshop executive summary in 2014 .

106
00:14:54,104 --> 00:15:03,104
It is difficult to summarize the current practices of obstetrical care in the management of the periviable pregnancy given its continued rapid evolution.

107
00:15:03,104 --> 00:15:11,779
Moreover, it appears that maternal and neonatal interventions are shifting during the 22- to 23-week gestational age period.

108
00:15:11,779 --> 00:15:25,917
Data from the National Center for Health Statistics show that rates of at least one maternal or neonatal intervention were 38.9 percent and 78.3 percent for 22 and 23 weeks’ gestation, respectively .

109
00:15:25,917 --> 00:15:33,617
In this uncertain environment, individualized, patient-centered care with a multidisciplinary team remains essential.

110
00:15:33,617 --> 00:15:42,054
This is particularly relevant given that nearly 20 percent of women may have serious morbidity with delivery during the periviable period .

111
00:15:42,054 --> 00:15:52,254
For cases with threatened periviable delivery, neonatal and perinatal consultation aids informed decision-making and helps form expectations for the family.

112
00:15:52,254 --> 00:16:01,429
For fetuses with a life-limiting condition, such as extreme prematurity, perinatal palliative care is a strategy that emphasizes comfort .

113
00:16:01,429 --> 00:16:08,667
Care team members can include obstetric and neonatal professionals, chaplaincy, and mental health specialists.

114
00:16:08,667 --> 00:16:11,354
# Late-preterm Birth.

115
00:16:11,354 --> 00:16:20,142
As discussed, neonates born between 34 and 36 weeks’ gestation account for more than 70 percent of all preterm births .

116
00:16:20,142 --> 00:16:30,229
Most of the increase in the total preterm birth rate for the United States 2018 to 2019 was among neonates born at 34 to 36 weeks.

117
00:16:30,229 --> 00:16:44,717
To estimate the risks associated with late-preterm births, investigators analyzed neonatal mortality and morbidity rates at 34, 35, and 36 weeks compared with those of births at term at Parkland Hospital .

118
00:16:44,717 --> 00:16:54,229
Approximately 3 percent of all births during the study period were between 24 and 32 weeks, and 9 percent were during the late-preterm period.

119
00:16:54,229 --> 00:17:01,567
Thus, and similar to the national rates, late-preterm births accounted for three fourths of all preterm births.

120
00:17:01,567 --> 00:17:09,617
Approximately 80 percent of these resulted from idiopathic spontaneous preterm labor or prematurely ruptured membranes .

121
00:17:09,617 --> 00:17:15,367
Other obstetrical complications were implicated in the remaining 20 percent of cases.

122
00:17:15,367 --> 00:17:22,092
Rates of morbidity and mortality were greater in these late-preterm newborns compared with rates in term ones .

123
00:17:22,092 --> 00:17:29,892
Similarly, Tomashek (2007) reported higher neonatal mortality rates for late- preterm newborns.

124
00:17:29,892 --> 00:17:35,742
Rates of adverse neurodevelopment outcomes also are increased in these late-preterm infants .

125
00:17:35,742 --> 00:17:43,392
Taken together, these findings suggest that a health-care focus on prematurity should include these late-preterm births.

126
00:17:43,392 --> 00:17:45,942
Figure 45-2.

127
00:17:45,942 --> 00:17:52,204
Distribution of preterm births by gestational age in the United States in 2019.

128
00:17:52,204 --> 00:17:54,867
Figure 45-3.

129
00:17:54,867 --> 00:18:03,117
Obstetrical complications associated with 21,771 late-preterm births at Parkland Hospital.

130
00:18:03,117 --> 00:18:05,842
Table 45-5.

131
00:18:05,842 --> 00:18:14,117
Neonatal Outcomes in Live Births Delivered Late Preterm Compared with Referent Group at 39 Weeks Causes Of Preterm Birth.

132
00:18:14,117 --> 00:18:34,354
Four direct causes for preterm births in the United States include (1) spontaneous unexplained preterm labor with intact membranes, (2) idiopathic preterm prelabor rupture of membranes, (3) delivery for maternal or fetal indications, and (4) twins and higher-order multifetal births.

133
00:18:34,354 --> 00:18:46,992
Of all preterm births, 30 to 35 percent are indicated, 40 to 45 percent are due to spontaneous preterm labor, and 30 to 35 percent follow preterm membrane rupture .

134
00:18:46,992 --> 00:18:52,604
More than 98 percent of triplets and 60 percent of twins are born preterm .

135
00:18:52,604 --> 00:18:58,729
At term, a common parturition pathway is activated physiologically and leads to labor.

136
00:18:58,729 --> 00:19:06,317
For preterm labor, several disease processes are thought to activate one or more of the components of this common pathway .

137
00:19:06,317 --> 00:19:11,504
Put another way, preterm labor is not simply labor that starts too soon.

138
00:19:11,504 --> 00:19:26,292
This paradigm is supported by the rationale that causes of preterm birth have multiple, often interacting, antecedents and contributing factors that lead to a heterogenous syndrome (Deindl, 2020; Esplin, 2016).

139
00:19:26,292 --> 00:19:31,304
Analogous to other complex disease processes, multiple coexistent

140
00:19:31,468 --> 00:19:44,905
genetic alterations, bacterial and viral colonization, and diverse environmental factors may lead to preterm birth (Bayar, 2020; Mekonnen, 2021; Volozonoka, 2020).

141
00:19:44,855 --> 00:20:04,405
These multiple factors continue to pose challenges in identifying predictive biomarkers in blood, urine, or cervicovaginal fluid, despite significant technical advances in multiomic approaches (Ghaemi, 2021; Jehan, 2020; Peterson, 2020; Parry, 2020).

142
00:20:04,405 --> 00:20:07,680
# Spontaneous Preterm Labor.

143
00:20:07,680 --> 00:20:16,368
Pregnancies with spontaneous preterm labor yet intact fetal membranes must be distinguished from those complicated by ruptured membranes.

144
00:20:16,368 --> 00:20:22,143
Even so, spontaneous preterm labor does not constitute a homogeneous group.

145
00:20:22,143 --> 00:20:27,780
Evidence suggests it is a syndrome attributable to multiple pathological processes .

146
00:20:27,780 --> 00:20:44,418
Among the more common associated findings are multifetal pregnancy, intrauterine infection, bleeding, placental infarction, premature cervical dilation, cervical insufficiency, hydramnios, uterine fundal abnormalities, and fetal anomalies.

147
00:20:44,418 --> 00:20:53,180
Severe maternal illness from infections, autoimmune diseases, and gestational hypertension also elevate preterm labor risks.

148
00:20:53,180 --> 00:21:02,018
These processes culminate in a common end point of premature cervical dilation, effacement, and activation of uterine contractions.

149
00:21:02,018 --> 00:21:13,668
Importantly, the actual process of preterm labor should be considered a final step stemming from progressive or acute changes that could be initiated days or even weeks before labor onset.

150
00:21:13,668 --> 00:21:20,405
Diverse pathways to instigate parturition exist and are dependent on the etiology of preterm birth .

151
00:21:20,405 --> 00:21:28,543
Major causes include uterine overdistention, premature cervical changes, infection, and maternal–fetal stress.

152
00:21:28,543 --> 00:21:33,343
Genetic and environmental factors likely contribute to each mechanism.

153
00:21:33,343 --> 00:21:36,055
Figure 45-4.

154
00:21:36,055 --> 00:21:41,205
Proposed mechanisms of disease implicated in spontaneous preterm labor.

155
00:21:41,205 --> 00:21:46,393
Genetic and environmental factors are likely contributors to each mechanism.

156
00:21:46,393 --> 00:21:53,443
Uterine Overdistention Multifetal pregnancy and hydramnios are well-recognized risks for preterm birth.

157
00:21:53,443 --> 00:21:58,618
With these, uterine overdistention imparts greater stress on the myometrium.

158
00:21:58,618 --> 00:22:08,305
In nonhuman primates, inflation of intraamnionic balloons can stimulate uterine contractility, preterm labor, and an “inflammatory pulse.

159
00:22:08,305 --> 00:22:15,155
Similar inflammatory responses have been observed in the amnion of women with polyhydramnios and twins .

160
00:22:15,155 --> 00:22:22,943
Early uterine overdistention likely acts to initiate expression of contraction-associated proteins in the myometrium.

161
00:22:22,943 --> 00:22:36,180
These genes include those coding for gap-junction proteins, oxytocin receptors, and prostaglandin synthase (Korita, 2002; Lyall, 2002; Sooranna, 2004).

162
00:22:36,180 --> 00:22:42,980
Excessive uterine distention also leads to early activation of the placental–fetal endocrine cascade .

163
00:22:42,980 --> 00:23:00,080
Taken together, mechanical stress-induced distension and activation of endocrine cascades both lead to premature activation of inflammatory responses that result in uterine contractile activation (Gomez-Lopez, 2014; Stephen, 2015).

164
00:23:00,080 --> 00:23:06,243
A possible compensatory response to uterine distention is tissue remodeling and muscle growth .

165
00:23:06,243 --> 00:23:12,855
Cervical Dysfunction In most cases, premature cervical remodeling precedes labor onset.

166
00:23:12,855 --> 00:23:20,718
In some instances, cervical dysfunction of the epithelia or its stromal extracellular matrix is the underlying cause .

167
00:23:20,718 --> 00:23:27,218
Importantly, an intact cervical epithelial barrier is critical to prevent ascending infection.

168
00:23:27,218 --> 00:23:41,005
For example, loss of hyaluronan in cervical epithelia or colonization of group B streptococcus (GBS) enhances risk of preterm birth (Akgul, 2014; Coleman, 2021).

169
00:23:41,005 --> 00:23:48,743
GBS has the unique ability to synthesize the hyaluronan-degrading enzyme hyaluronidase to aid bacterial ascension .

170
00:23:48,743 --> 00:23:53,243
Second, the mechanical competence of the cervix can be reduced.

171
00:23:53,243 --> 00:24:08,230
Genetic mutations in components of collagen and elastic fibers or proteins required for their assembly can be risk factors for cervical insufficiency and preterm birth (Nallasamy, 2017; Volozonoka, 2020).

172
00:24:08,230 --> 00:24:18,168
Infection Of all the mechanisms listed in Figure 45-4, only intraamnionic infection has been causally linked to spontaneous preterm delivery .

173
00:24:18,168 --> 00:24:24,655
Bacteria can gain access to intrauterine tissues through (1) transplacental transfer of

174
00:24:24,820 --> 00:24:35,257
maternal systemic infection, (2) flow of infection via the fallopian tubes, or (3) ascending infection with bacteria from the vagina and cervix.

175
00:24:35,207 --> 00:24:46,532
Because the lower pole of the fetal membrane–decidual junction is contiguous with the cervical canal orifice, this anatomical arrangement provides a passageway for microorganisms.

176
00:24:46,532 --> 00:24:58,382
Ascending infection is considered to be the most common entry route where microorganisms colonize the cervix, decidua, and possibly the membranes, and then may enter the amnionic sac.

177
00:24:58,382 --> 00:25:05,507
Colonization with an infectious agent has been detected in 25 to 40 percent of all preterm deliveries .

178
00:25:05,507 --> 00:25:14,382
In some instances, histological evidence of microbial inflammation is found in the fetal membranes, decidua, or umbilical cord.

179
00:25:14,382 --> 00:25:18,395
Other cases are deemed sterile intraamnionic inflammation.

180
00:25:18,395 --> 00:25:33,245
Despite the substantial association of infection with preterm birth, the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying inflammation-driven preterm birth remain undefined (Cappelletti, 2020; Purisch, 2017).

181
00:25:33,245 --> 00:25:41,345
Current data suggest that microbial invasion of the reproductive tract is sufficient to induce infection-mediated preterm birth.

182
00:25:41,345 --> 00:25:47,920
The level and kinetics of inflammation can vary between a polymicrobial or single-microbe exposure .

183
00:25:47,920 --> 00:25:56,395
Affected women are more likely to develop clinical chorioamnionitis and rupture of membranes compared with women whose cultures are sterile.

184
00:25:56,395 --> 00:26:03,357
Moreover, their neonates are also more likely to have perinatal complications such as neonatal sepsis .

185
00:26:03,357 --> 00:26:18,932
Although the clinical course is more severe when intraamnionic infection is obvious, inflammation in the absence of detectable intraamnionic microorganisms—termed sterile intraamnionic inflammation—is also a risk factor for an inflammatory response .

186
00:26:18,932 --> 00:26:29,270
In sum, the earlier the onset of preterm labor, the greater the likelihood of underlying infection (Goldenberg, 2000; Goncalves, 2002).

187
00:26:29,270 --> 00:26:43,870
Paradoxically, the incidence of culture-positive amnionic fluid collected by amniocentesis during spontaneous labor at term is similar to that with preterm labor (Gomez, 1994; Romero, 1993).

188
00:26:43,870 --> 00:26:55,582
It has been suggested that at term, amnionic fluid is infiltrated by bacteria as a consequence of labor, whereas in preterm pregnancies, bacteria represent an inciting cause.

189
00:26:55,582 --> 00:27:04,395
Thus, fetal infection, as defined by bacteria detected within amnionic fluid, has differing etiologies and consequences.

190
00:27:04,395 --> 00:27:10,995
With chorioamnionitis, microbes may invade only maternal tissue and not amnionic fluid.

191
00:27:10,995 --> 00:27:18,570
Despite this, endotoxins can stimulate amnionic cells to secrete cytokines that enter amnionic fluid.

192
00:27:18,570 --> 00:27:32,345
This scenario may serve to explain the apparently contradictory observations concerning an association between amnionic fluid cytokines and preterm labor in cases in which microbes are not detected in the amnionic fluid.

193
00:27:32,345 --> 00:27:34,945
Inflammatory Responses.

194
00:27:34,945 --> 00:27:40,245
These responses drive the pathogenesis of infection-induced preterm labor.

195
00:27:40,245 --> 00:27:49,982
Lipopolysaccharide (LPS) or other toxins elaborated by bacteria are recognized by receptors such as toll-like receptors (TLRs) .

196
00:27:49,982 --> 00:28:01,782
These receptors are present on mononuclear phagocytes, decidual cells, cervical epithelia, and trophoblasts (Gonzalez, 2007; Holmlund, 2002).

197
00:28:01,782 --> 00:28:11,007
The expression of TLRs on maternal immune cells is necessary to recognize inflammatory stimuli in inflammationmediated preterm birth .

198
00:28:11,007 --> 00:28:22,070
Activation of TLRs induces a signaling cascade that activates production of chemokines such as interleukin 8 (IL-8) and cytokines such as IL-1β.

199
00:28:22,070 --> 00:28:26,770
Activation also recruits immune cells into the reproductive tract.

200
00:28:26,770 --> 00:28:34,382
Cytokines are produced by immune cells and by cells within the cervix, decidua, membranes, or fetus itself.

201
00:28:34,382 --> 00:29:02,695
LPS-induced production of IL-1β in turn promotes a series of responses that include (1) increased synthesis of others, that is, IL-6, IL-8, and tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-α); (2) proliferation, activation, and migration of leukocytes; (3) modifications in extracellular matrix proteins; and (4) mitogenic and cytotoxic effects such as fever and acute-phase response .

202
00:29:02,695 --> 00:29:12,307
In many tissues, including myometrium, decidua, and amnion, IL-1β also promotes prostaglandin formation that induces

203
00:29:12,460 --> 00:29:20,010
cervical ripening and loss of myometrial quiescence (Challis, 2002; Keelan, 2003).

204
00:29:19,960 --> 00:29:37,322
The importance of prostaglandins to infection-mediated preterm birth is supported by the observation that prostaglandin inhibitors can reduce the rate of LPS-induced preterm birth in both the mouse and nonhuman primate (Gravett, 2007; Timmons, 2014).

205
00:29:37,322 --> 00:29:43,685
Inhibition of cyclooxygenase 2 prevents inflammation-mediated preterm labor in the mouse.

206
00:29:43,685 --> 00:29:49,897
For these reasons, clinical approaches described on page 806 are being explored.

207
00:29:49,897 --> 00:30:03,247
Proteases such as matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) also are induced by inflammatory cytokines and function to break down extracellular matrix components such as collagen or elastic fibers.

208
00:30:03,247 --> 00:30:08,197
This disrupts the structural integrity of fetal membranes or the cervix.

209
00:30:08,197 --> 00:30:24,947
Current evidence from animal and human studies suggests that many aspects of infection-mediated preterm birth differ from pathways that regulate term parturition (Hamilton, 2012; Shynlova, 2013a,b; Willcockson, 2018).

210
00:30:24,947 --> 00:30:27,260
Origin of Cytokines.

211
00:30:27,260 --> 00:30:32,235
Secretion of uterine cytokines is likely important for preterm labor.

212
00:30:32,235 --> 00:30:45,310
Cytokines produced in maternal decidua and myometrium have effects confined to that side, whereas cytokines produced in the membranes or in cells within the amnionic fluid will not be transferred to maternal tissues.

213
00:30:45,310 --> 00:30:53,535
In general, resident and invading leukocytes produce the bulk of cytokines in cases of inflammation resulting from infection.

214
00:30:53,535 --> 00:31:00,310
The presence of cytokines in amnionic fluid and their association with preterm labor is well documented.

215
00:31:00,310 --> 00:31:07,297
But, their exact cellular origin—with or without recoverable microorganisms—is not well defined.

216
00:31:07,297 --> 00:31:16,685
Amnionic fluid cytokines are most likely secreted by mononuclear phagocytes or neutrophils activated and recruited into the amnionic fluid .

217
00:31:16,685 --> 00:31:30,247
Thus, the amount of amnionic fluid IL-1β would be determined by the number of leukocytes recruited, their activational status, or the effect of amnionic fluid constituents on their IL-1β secretion rate.

218
00:31:30,247 --> 00:31:32,172
Microbiota.

219
00:31:32,172 --> 00:31:50,247
Mucosal immunity and barrier function of the cervicovaginal epithelia, the vaginal microbiota composition, and their interplay among differing populations are major research topics (Elovitz, 2019; Fettweis, 2019; Serrano, 2019).

220
00:31:50,247 --> 00:32:02,872
To explore these, advanced genomic analysis techniques are used and show that the nonpregnant vagina hosts a complex microbial community (Gajer, 2012; White, 2011).

221
00:32:02,872 --> 00:32:07,547
And, the vaginal microbiome changes during normal pregnancy .

222
00:32:07,547 --> 00:32:14,372
Specifically, the diversity of microbe populations are reduced during pregnancy and become more stable.

223
00:32:14,372 --> 00:32:29,810
Some but not all studies report an increased population of certain microbes—for example, Gardnerella vaginalis and Ureaplasma urealyticum—in women with preterm birth (Donders, 2009; Nelson, 2014).

224
00:32:29,810 --> 00:32:39,822
In contrast, Schuster and coworkers (2020) reported that asymptomatic vaginal Candida colonization was not associated with preterm birth.

225
00:32:39,822 --> 00:32:48,172
Differences in populations studied, preterm birth definitions, and data analysis complicate interpretation of these data.

226
00:32:48,172 --> 00:32:55,672
Currently, the strongest evidence for the role of the microbiome in preterm birth relates to the vaginal microbiota.

227
00:32:55,672 --> 00:33:04,347
The most consistent finding across almost all studies is the benefit of a vaginal microbiota characterized by Lactobacillus crispatus .

228
00:33:04,347 --> 00:33:12,260
Aagaard and colleagues (2014) suggested that the placenta contains a microbiome akin to the oral microbiome.

229
00:33:12,260 --> 00:33:20,397
Subsequent studies, however, have failed to confirm this (Lauder, 2016; Theis, 2019).

230
00:33:20,397 --> 00:33:33,060
Recently, de Goffau and associates (2019) found no evidence of bacteria in most of 537 placental samples from both complicated and uncomplicated pregnancies.

231
00:33:33,060 --> 00:33:43,497
Almost all samples pointed to acquisition of bacteria during labor and delivery or to contamination of laboratory reagents with the exception of Streptococcus agalactiae.

232
00:33:43,497 --> 00:33:48,422
Thus, the human placenta does not appear to have a relevant microbiome .

233
00:33:48,422 --> 00:33:52,210
# Preterm Prelabor Rupture of Membranes.

234
00:33:52,210 --> 00:34:06,510
Previously referred to as preterm premature rupture of membranes, preterm prelabor rupture of membranes (PPROM) defines spontaneous membrane rupture before 37 completed weeks and before labor onset .

235
00:34:06,676 --> 00:34:22,001
Such rupture likely has various causes, but intrauterine infection, oxidative stress–induced DNA damage, and premature cellular senescence are major predisposing events (Dutta, 2016; Mercer, 2003).

236
00:34:21,951 --> 00:34:33,138
Associated risk factors are similar to those at term and include lower socioeconomical status, low body mass index, nutritional deficiencies, and cigarette smoking.

237
00:34:33,138 --> 00:34:39,138
Women with PPROM carry an enhanced risk for recurrence during a subsequent pregnancy .

238
00:34:39,138 --> 00:34:44,038
Despite these known risk factors, none are identified in many cases.

239
00:34:44,038 --> 00:34:49,963
Bacterial cultures of amnionic fluid support a role for infection in PPROM.

240
00:34:49,963 --> 00:34:59,738
One review of 18 studies and almost 1500 women with PPROM found that bacteria were isolated from amnionic fluid in a third of cases .

241
00:34:59,738 --> 00:35:05,401
Microbiome-mediated preterm birth and PPROM are current research areas .

242
00:35:05,401 --> 00:35:10,126
One goal is to identify early risk markers for PPROM.

243
00:35:10,126 --> 00:35:24,626
Molecular Changes In pregnancies with PPROM, the amnion exhibits a higher degree of cell death and more apoptosis markers than in term amnion (ArechavaletaVelasco, 2002; Fortunato, 2003).

244
00:35:24,626 --> 00:35:33,576
In vitro studies indicate that apoptosis is likely regulated by bacterial endotoxin, IL-1β, and TNF-α.

245
00:35:33,576 --> 00:35:46,276
In addition, oxidative stress initiated by events other than infection can induce DNA damage, premature senescence, and subsequent inflammation and proteolysis that leads to PPROM .

246
00:35:46,276 --> 00:35:53,601
With membrane rupture, thrombin activity rises, which activates MMPs and prostaglandin synthesis.

247
00:35:53,601 --> 00:36:06,701
Studies by Mogami (2013) provide a mechanism by which bacterial endotoxin or TNF-α elicits release of fetal fibronectin (fFN) by amnion epithelial cells.

248
00:36:06,701 --> 00:36:14,151
The fFN then binds toll-like receptor 4 in the amnion mesenchymal cells to activate signaling cascades.

249
00:36:14,151 --> 00:36:26,851
These result in augmented prostaglandin E (PGE2) synthesis and elevated activity of MMPs. Increasing prostaglandin levels promote cervical ripening and uterine contractions.

250
00:36:26,851 --> 00:36:34,388
Greater MMP concentrations allow collagen breakdown in the fetal membranes, resulting in premature rupture.

251
00:36:34,388 --> 00:36:42,588
Last, proteins involved in collagen synthesis or promoting its tensile strength are altered in membranes with premature rupture .

252
00:36:42,588 --> 00:36:51,013
Recently, Blois and associates (2020) introduced the concept of galectins, a family of glycan-binding proteins.

253
00:36:51,013 --> 00:36:58,976
They may serve as a mediator in fetal-maternal immune tolerance and work to prevent microbial infections that lead to preterm birth.

254
00:36:58,976 --> 00:37:08,213
Both preterm labor and PPROM arise from distinct pathophysiological pathways but share inflammation as a common underlying mechanism.

255
00:37:08,213 --> 00:37:15,376
Excellent reviews are presented by Menon (2020) and Diemert (2020) and their coworkers.

256
00:37:15,376 --> 00:37:18,151
# Maternal-Fetal Stress.

257
00:37:18,151 --> 00:37:26,801
In this context, stress is defined as a condition that disturbs the normal physiological or psychological functioning of an individual.

258
00:37:26,801 --> 00:37:33,413
Since the 1940s, the association of maternal stress with birth outcomes has been explored.

259
00:37:33,413 --> 00:37:38,426
Results vary partly because of methodological difference in measuring stress .

260
00:37:38,426 --> 00:37:52,276
Quantitative measure is difficult, but considerable evidence shows that psychosocial stress in the form of racial discrimination— especially in black populations—results in poor health outcomes, including preterm birth .

261
00:37:52,276 --> 00:38:05,363
Psychological duress can include childhood stress, depression, or posttraumatic stress syndrome (Gillespie, 2017; Goldstein, 2017; Venkatesh, 2016).

262
00:38:05,363 --> 00:38:15,101
In one review of more than 50 studies, a significant link was found between low birthweight and preterm birth in women impacted by intimate partner violence .

263
00:38:15,101 --> 00:38:23,113
One potential mechanism for stress-induced preterm birth is premature activation of the placental–adrenal endocrine axis.

264
00:38:23,113 --> 00:38:29,488
Another mechanism by which stress may translate to preterm birth is premature cellular senescence.

265
00:38:29,488 --> 00:38:38,738
As part of normal physiology, aging of fetal and decidual cells precipitates release of uterotonic signals for uterine activation at term.

266
00:38:38,738 --> 00:38:46,151
The interplay between preterm birth and maternal stress was recently reviewed by Hong and colleagues (2021).

267
00:38:46,151 --> 00:38:48,376
Contributing Factors.

268
00:38:48,376 --> 00:38:59,601
Several factors associated with preterm birth include pregnancy factors, lifestyle and behaviors, genetic and demographic features, and aspects of obstetrical history .

269
00:38:59,764 --> 00:39:04,051
Disentangling association from causation remains a dilemma.

270
00:39:04,001 --> 00:39:06,701
Table 45-6.

271
00:39:06,701 --> 00:39:12,764
Factors That May Contribute to the Genesis of Preterm Labor # Prior Preterm Birth.

272
00:39:12,764 --> 00:39:18,001
The most important risk factor for preterm labor is a prior preterm birth.

273
00:39:18,001 --> 00:39:23,626
Data from nearly 16,000 women delivered at Parkland Hospital are instructive .

274
00:39:23,626 --> 00:39:33,364
Namely, the recurrent preterm delivery risk for women with a preterm first delivery was threefold greater than that of women whose first neonate was born at term.

275
00:39:33,364 --> 00:39:40,176
More than a third of women whose first two newborns were preterm subsequently delivered a third preterm newborn.

276
00:39:40,176 --> 00:39:48,176
Most—70 percent—of the recurrent births occurred within 2 weeks of the gestational age of the prior preterm delivery.

277
00:39:48,176 --> 00:39:52,076
The causes of prior preterm delivery also recurred.

278
00:39:52,076 --> 00:40:00,639
Although women with prior preterm births are clearly at risk for recurrence, they represented only 10 percent of the total preterm births.

279
00:40:00,639 --> 00:40:09,276
Expressed another way, 90 percent of the preterm births at Parkland Hospital could not be predicted based on a history of preterm birth.

280
00:40:09,276 --> 00:40:14,089
Others have confirmed the importance of prior spontaneous preterm birth .

281
00:40:14,089 --> 00:40:21,751
Moreover, prior indicated preterm birth was strongly associated with subsequent spontaneous preterm birth.

282
00:40:21,751 --> 00:40:27,364
Variable definitions of spontaneous and indicated may explain this association.

283
00:40:27,364 --> 00:40:41,151
Ultimately, risk of recurrent preterm birth is influenced by three factors: the frequency of prior preterm deliveries, severity as measured by gestational age, and the order in which the prior preterm delivery occurred .

284
00:40:41,151 --> 00:40:50,001
That is, an individual woman’s risk for recurrent preterm birth is influenced by her past number and sequence of preterm and term births.

285
00:40:50,001 --> 00:41:03,076
For example, a risk of recurrent preterm birth for a gravida 3 para 2 woman with a prior preterm birth followed by a term birth is less than that for a woman with a prior term birth followed by preterm birth.

286
00:41:03,076 --> 00:41:10,264
Thus, the influence of reproductive history has a profound prognostic significance for risk of recurrence.

287
00:41:10,264 --> 00:41:16,089
This may also influence the supposed benefit attributed to interventions described later.

288
00:41:16,089 --> 00:41:18,751
# Pregnancy Factors.

289
00:41:18,751 --> 00:41:25,551
Of these, threatened abortion in early pregnancy is associated with higher rates of later adverse outcomes.

290
00:41:25,551 --> 00:41:34,464
Weiss (2004) reported outcomes in nearly 14,000 women with vaginal bleeding at 6 to 13 weeks’ gestation.

291
00:41:34,464 --> 00:41:43,451
Both light and heavy bleeding were associated with subsequent preterm labor, placental abruption, and pregnancy loss before 24 weeks.

292
00:41:43,451 --> 00:41:48,189
Birth defects in the fetus also may predispose to preterm birth.

293
00:41:48,189 --> 00:42:00,026
In a secondary analysis of data from the First- and Second-Trimester Evaluation of Risk (FASTER) trial, birth defects were associated with preterm birth and low-birthweight neonates .

294
00:42:00,026 --> 00:42:04,389
Multifetal gestation is another well-recognized associate.

295
00:42:04,389 --> 00:42:13,126
Importantly, preterm delivery continues to be the major cause of the excessive perinatal morbidity and mortality for these pregnancies.

296
00:42:13,126 --> 00:42:15,851
# Lifestyle Factors.

297
00:42:15,851 --> 00:42:26,864
Extremes of maternal weight—both underweight and obese mothers—have an enhanced risk of preterm birth (Cnattingius, 2013; Girsen, 2016).

298
00:42:26,864 --> 00:42:41,714
Other maternal factors implicated include young or advanced maternal age, poverty, short stature, and vitamin C deficiency (Casanueva, 2005; Goldenberg, 2008; Leveno, 2009).

299
00:42:41,714 --> 00:42:50,014
Cigarette smoking, inadequate maternal weight gain, and illicit drug use affect the incidence and outcome of lowbirthweight neonates .

300
00:42:50,014 --> 00:42:56,301
Studies of work and physical activity related to preterm birth have yielded conflicting results .

301
00:42:56,301 --> 00:43:05,676
Some evidence suggests that working long hours, fixed night shifts, and hard physical labor are probably linked to a higher risk of preterm birth .

302
00:43:05,676 --> 00:43:22,126
However, aerobic exercise in normal-weight women with uncomplicated singleton pregnancies appears to be safe and not associated with preterm birth (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020f; Di Mascio, 2016).

303
00:43:22,126 --> 00:43:30,414
One metaanalysis of physical activity found that leisure-time physical activity was associated with a reduced risk of preterm birth .

304
00:43:30,414 --> 00:43:38,564
Because of the observational nature of many of the studies with the potential for confounding, the American College of Obstetricians and

305
00:43:38,716 --> 00:43:45,778
Gynecologists (2020b) has offered guidance for employment considerations during pregnancy.

306
00:43:45,728 --> 00:43:54,916
It is emphasized that accommodations that allow a woman to keep working are the most reliable way to guarantee pay, benefits, and job protection.

307
00:43:54,916 --> 00:43:57,478
# Genetic Factors.

308
00:43:57,478 --> 00:44:04,078
Evidence for a genetic component to preterm birth is supported by familial recurrent preterm births .

309
00:44:04,078 --> 00:44:11,291
Genome-wide studies have identified genetic variants associated with gestational duration and preterm birth .

310
00:44:11,291 --> 00:44:18,778
Common variants in EBF1, EEFSEC, and AGTR2 genes have an association with preterm birth.

311
00:44:18,778 --> 00:44:27,866
Data sets, however, included only women with European ancestry, and thus future efforts will ideally include other populations.

312
00:44:27,866 --> 00:44:38,616
Recent large-scale transcriptome studies have identified additional loci associated with gestation length (Marinić, 2021; Sakabe, 2020).

313
00:44:38,616 --> 00:44:41,478
# Periodontal Disease.

314
00:44:41,478 --> 00:44:49,266
Gingivitis is a chronic anaerobic inflammation that affects as many as 50 percent of pregnant women in the United States .

315
00:44:49,266 --> 00:44:56,903
One metaanalysis of 17 studies showed that periodontal disease was significantly associated with preterm birth .

316
00:44:56,903 --> 00:45:09,353
To better study this relationship, 813 pregnant women between 13 and 17 weeks’ gestation who had periodontal disease were randomly assigned to treatment during pregnancy or postpartum.

317
00:45:09,353 --> 00:45:17,103
Treatment during pregnancy improved periodontal disease, however, it failed to significantly alter preterm birth rates .

318
00:45:17,103 --> 00:45:25,503
A workshop of the European Federation of Periodontology and the American Academy of Periodontology reaffirmed this position .

319
00:45:25,503 --> 00:45:28,416
# Interpregnancy Interval.

320
00:45:28,416 --> 00:45:39,753
In one metaanalysis, intervals <18 months and >59 months were associated with greater risks for both preterm birth and small-for-gestational-age newborns .

321
00:45:39,753 --> 00:45:45,278
The causal effect of short interpregnancy intervals, however, has been questioned .

322
00:45:45,278 --> 00:46:00,203
A recent cohort study of more than three million births across four countries, including the United States, found that associations between interpregnancy intervals and preterm birth are modified by whether the previous pregnancy was preterm .

323
00:46:00,203 --> 00:46:02,391
# Infection.

324
00:46:02,391 --> 00:46:12,228
Antimicrobial Prophylaxis As discussed on page 789, a link between some cases of preterm birth and infection seems irrefutable .

325
00:46:12,228 --> 00:46:20,503
In several studies, antimicrobial treatment has been given to prevent preterm labor that was thought to stem from microbial invasion.

326
00:46:20,503 --> 00:46:24,716
Some of these strategies have targeted Mycoplasma species.

327
00:46:24,716 --> 00:46:42,641
First, Andrews and associates (2006) reported results of a randomized trial in which they provided a course of azithromycin plus metronidazole every 4 months to 241 nonpregnant women whose last pregnancy resulted in spontaneous delivery before 34 weeks.

328
00:46:42,641 --> 00:46:50,916
Approximately 80 percent of the women with subsequent pregnancies had received study drug within 6 months of their subsequent conception.

329
00:46:50,916 --> 00:46:57,303
Such interconceptional antimicrobial treatment did not reduce the rate of recurrent preterm birth.

330
00:46:57,303 --> 00:47:06,653
From a subgroup analysis of this data, Tita and coworkers (2007) concluded that such use of antimicrobials may be harmful.

331
00:47:06,653 --> 00:47:21,103
In another randomized study, 2661 women were given placebo or metronidazole plus erythromycin between 20 and 24 weeks’ gestation followed by ampicillin plus metronidazole during labor .

332
00:47:21,103 --> 00:47:28,178
This antimicrobial regimen did not reduce the rate of preterm birth or histological chorioamnionitis.

333
00:47:28,178 --> 00:47:38,066
As discussed later, antibiotic prophylaxis to prevent preterm birth is not currently recommended in women with preterm labor and intact membranes .

334
00:47:38,066 --> 00:47:47,841
Bacterial Vaginosis In this condition, normal, hydrogen peroxide–producing, lactobacilluspredominant vaginal flora is replaced with anaerobes.

335
00:47:47,841 --> 00:48:05,491
For nearly 40 years, bacterial vaginosis has been recognized to be associated with spontaneous abortion, preterm labor, rupture of membranes, chorioamnionitis, and amnionic fluid infection (Gravett, 1986; Hillier, 1995).

336
00:48:05,491 --> 00:48:10,491
Unfortunately, screening and treatment have not prevented preterm birth .

337
00:48:10,491 --> 00:48:24,166
Moreover, microbial resistance or antimicrobial-induced change in the vaginal flora results from regimens intended to eliminate bacterial vaginosis (Beigi, 2004; Carey, 2005).

338
00:48:24,166 --> 00:48:26,078
Covid-19.

339
00:48:26,078 --> 00:48:29,778
Early reports suggested an increase in rates of

340
00:48:29,932 --> 00:48:33,682
preterm birth in pregnant women with COVID-19.

341
00:48:33,632 --> 00:48:46,257
However, population-level data show conflicting results on the changes, if any, in preterm birth rates (Berghella, 2020; Hedermann, 2021; Main, 2021).

342
00:48:46,257 --> 00:49:00,857
At Parkland Hospital, Adhikari and colleagues (2020) did not identify higher rates of preterm birth in 252 women with COVID-19 compared with 3122 gravidas who tested negative.

343
00:49:00,857 --> 00:49:07,107
However, women with severe infection are often delivered preterm for maternal indications .

344
00:49:07,107 --> 00:49:08,982
Diagnosis.

345
00:49:08,982 --> 00:49:11,182
# Symptoms.

346
00:49:11,182 --> 00:49:20,469
Contractions, pelvic pressure, menstrual-like cramps, watery vaginal discharge, and lower back pain are typical symptoms of preterm labor.

347
00:49:20,469 --> 00:49:26,569
These can also be seen with normal pregnancy and may be minimized by patients and providers.

348
00:49:26,569 --> 00:49:34,907
Early differentiation between true and false labor is difficult —especially before demonstrable cervical effacement and dilation.

349
00:49:34,907 --> 00:49:40,232
Uterine activity alone can be misleading because of Braxton Hicks contractions .

350
00:49:40,232 --> 00:49:59,619
Accordingly, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) defines preterm labor to be regular contractions accompanied by a change in cervical dilation, effacement, or both or to be regular contractions and cervical dilation of at least 2 cm at initial presentation.

351
00:49:59,619 --> 00:50:16,957
Chao (2011) prospectively studied 843 women with a singleton fetus who presented to Parkland Hospital with preterm labor symptoms between 240/7 and 336/7 weeks, intact membranes, and cervical dilation <2 cm.

352
00:50:16,957 --> 00:50:23,657
Those whose cervix remained <2 cm were sent home with a diagnosis of false preterm labor.

353
00:50:23,657 --> 00:50:33,357
When analyzed against the general obstetrical population, women sent home had a similar rate of birth before 34 weeks—2 versus 1 percent.

354
00:50:33,357 --> 00:50:42,494
However, these women did have significantly higher rates of birth between 34 and 36 weeks—5 percent compared with 2 percent.

355
00:50:42,494 --> 00:50:54,782
Women with cervical dilation of 1 cm at discharge were significantly more likely to deliver before 34 weeks compared with women without cervical dilation—5 percent versus 1 percent.

356
00:50:54,782 --> 00:51:01,469
Almost 90 percent of the 1-cm group delivered within 21 days of the initial presentation.

357
00:51:01,469 --> 00:51:04,019
# Cervical Change.

358
00:51:04,019 --> 00:51:10,807
Asymptomatic cervical dilation after midpregnancy is suspected to be a preterm delivery risk factor.

359
00:51:10,807 --> 00:51:17,644
Multiparity alone is not sufficient to explain cervical dilation discovered early in the third trimester.

360
00:51:17,644 --> 00:51:30,782
Cook (1996) longitudinally evaluated cervical status with transvaginal sonography between 18 and 30 weeks’ gestation in nulliparas and multiparas who all subsequently gave birth at term.

361
00:51:30,782 --> 00:51:36,294
Cervical length and diameter were identical in both groups throughout these critical weeks.

362
00:51:36,294 --> 00:51:46,682
In a study from Parkland Hospital, routine digital cervical examinations were performed between 26 and 30 weeks in 185 asymptomatic women.

363
00:51:46,682 --> 00:51:54,407
Approximately 25 percent of women whose cervix was dilated 2 or 3 cm delivered before 34 weeks .

364
00:51:54,407 --> 00:52:01,257
Other investigators have verified cervical dilation as a predictor of increased preterm delivery risk .

365
00:52:01,257 --> 00:52:11,332
Although women with dilation and effacement in the third trimester are at greater risk for preterm birth, detection does not necessarily improve pregnancy outcome.

366
00:52:11,332 --> 00:52:25,907
In one randomized study, 2719 women undergoing routine cervical examinations at each prenatal visit were compared with 2721 gravidas in whom serial examinations were not performed.

367
00:52:25,907 --> 00:52:35,582
Knowledge of antenatal cervical dilation did not affect any pregnancy outcome related to preterm birth or the frequency of interventions for preterm labor .

368
00:52:35,582 --> 00:52:42,944
Thus, it seems that prenatal cervical examinations in asymptomatic women are neither beneficial nor harmful.

369
00:52:42,944 --> 00:52:52,332
Numerous cervical imaging technologies are under investigation for early, accurate prediction of cervical changes associated with preterm birth .

370
00:52:52,332 --> 00:52:55,694
# Ambulatory Uterine Monitoring.

371
00:52:55,694 --> 00:53:05,569
An external tocodynamometer belted around the abdomen and connected to an electronic waist recorder allows a woman to ambulate while uterine activity is recorded.

372
00:53:05,569 --> 00:53:09,032
Results are transmitted via telephone daily.

373
00:53:09,032 --> 00:53:16,744
Women are educated concerning signs and symptoms of preterm labor, and clinicians are kept apprised of their progress.

374
00:53:16,744 --> 00:53:21,532
The 1985 approval of this monitor by the U.S. Food and Drug

375
00:53:21,700 --> 00:53:26,600
Administration (FDA) prompted its widespread clinical use.

376
00:53:26,550 --> 00:53:39,500
Subsequently, it was proven that this expensive and time-consuming system did not reduce preterm birth rates (Collaborative Home Uterine Monitoring Study Group, 1995; Iams, 2002).

377
00:53:39,500 --> 00:53:44,487
Despite technology improvements, use of such monitoring is discouraged .

378
00:53:44,487 --> 00:53:46,875
# Biomarkers.

379
00:53:46,875 --> 00:53:54,137
Because clinical symptoms alone are not predictive of preterm birth, surrogate biomarkers have been evaluated.

380
00:53:54,137 --> 00:54:07,225
These include fetal fibronectin (fFN), phosphorylated insulin-like growth factor–binding protein 1 (phIGFBP-1), and placental alpha microglobulin 1 (PAMG-1).

381
00:54:07,225 --> 00:54:13,237
Their performance varies between studies and is influenced by demographic and other factors .

382
00:54:13,237 --> 00:54:26,200
Fetal Fibronectin This glycoprotein is produced in 20 different molecular forms by various cell types, including hepatocytes, fibroblasts, endothelial cells, and fetal amnion cells.

383
00:54:26,200 --> 00:54:38,725
Present in high concentrations in maternal blood and amnionic fluid, fFN is thought to function in intercellular adhesion during implantation and in maintenance of placental adherence to uterine decidua .

384
00:54:38,725 --> 00:54:50,000
Detected in cervicovaginal secretions in women who have normal pregnancies with intact membranes at term, fFN appears to reflect stromal remodeling of the cervix before labor.

385
00:54:50,000 --> 00:55:01,000
Qualitative and quantitative fFN levels are measured using enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays, and values exceeding 50 ng/mL are considered positive.

386
00:55:01,000 --> 00:55:06,350
Sample contamination by amnionic fluid and maternal blood should be avoided.

387
00:55:06,350 --> 00:55:18,850
Interventional studies based on fFN screening results in asymptomatic women have not demonstrated improved perinatal outcomes (Andrews, 2003; Esplin, 2017).

388
00:55:18,850 --> 00:55:27,525
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021c) does not recommend screening with fFN tests.

389
00:55:27,525 --> 00:55:38,262
In one review of 16 trials involving management based upon fFN results, the low quality of existing evidence and need for cost-effectiveness analyses were noted .

390
00:55:38,262 --> 00:55:41,225
# Cervical Length Measurement.

391
00:55:41,225 --> 00:55:48,425
Progressively shorter cervical canals assessed sonographically are associated with increased rates of preterm birth .

392
00:55:48,425 --> 00:55:54,200
The sonographic technique is described in Chapter 14 (p. 254).

393
00:55:54,200 --> 00:56:11,300
The Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2016b) has provided guidance for proper cervical length measurement and recommends that sonographers and practitioners obtain specific training through accreditation programs in the acquisition and interpretation of cervical length images.

394
00:56:11,300 --> 00:56:20,225
Transvaginal cervical sonography is not affected by maternal obesity, cervix position, or shadowing from the fetal presenting part.

395
00:56:20,225 --> 00:56:32,450
Because of the inability to easily distinguish the lower uterine segment from the cervix in early gestation, transvaginal cervical length assessment is typically performed after 16 weeks’ gestation.

396
00:56:32,450 --> 00:56:41,275
Such interrogation is currently limited to singleton gestations and not recommended for multifetal gestations outside of research trials .

397
00:56:41,275 --> 00:56:45,500
Indications for cervical length measurement are controversial.

398
00:56:45,500 --> 00:56:57,062
For those women with a history of prior spontaneous preterm birth, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2016b) recommends transvaginal cervical length screening.

399
00:56:57,062 --> 00:57:06,287
And, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021c) now also recommends screening for this indication.

400
00:57:06,287 --> 00:57:19,850
In women with singleton pregnancies but without a history of prior preterm birth, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine and the College view cervical length screening as reasonable yet acknowledges that this remains an area of debate.

401
00:57:19,850 --> 00:57:24,787
Biomarkers have been incorporated with hopes to improve screening performance.

402
00:57:24,787 --> 00:57:32,700
Esplin and colleagues (2017) prospectively studied 9410 nulliparas with singleton pregnancies.

403
00:57:32,700 --> 00:57:45,275
Universal screening of sonographically measured cervical length and quantitative measurement of vaginal fFN levels were evaluated as predictors of women who would spontaneously deliver before 37 weeks.

404
00:57:45,275 --> 00:57:49,712
These measures had poor predictive performance as a screening test.

405
00:57:49,712 --> 00:57:56,612
Based on these findings, routine use of these screening tests in a low-risk population is not recommended.

406
00:57:56,612 --> 00:58:02,812
Of other markers, PAMG-1 has emerged as superior to predict potential preterm

407
00:58:02,980 --> 00:58:14,017
birth compared with fFN, particularly among symptomatic women (Nikolova, 2018; Pirjani, 2019; Wing, 2017).

408
00:58:13,967 --> 00:58:25,992
Incorporating results of cervicovaginal fluid proteomic markers and microRNAs obtained from peripheral blood also has been assessed (Parry, 2020; Winger, 2020).

409
00:58:25,992 --> 00:58:32,267
Although evidence seems promising, any firm recommendations should await larger clinical trials .

410
00:58:32,267 --> 00:58:43,242
This is particularly important given the challenges with accuracy and utility of screening, especially in low-risk women, who represent most of the population with preterm birth.

411
00:58:43,242 --> 00:58:54,292
A second concern with screening is the efficacy of interventions to improve perinatal outcomes once cervical length screening, biomarkers, or both have isolated at-risk women.

412
00:58:54,292 --> 00:59:01,830
Cervical cerclage or vaginal progesterone have been evaluated, and their efficacy is described in the next section.

413
00:59:01,830 --> 00:59:13,067
Bloom and Leveno (2017) subsequently critiqued the use of transvaginal cervical length screening in low-risk women and the promulgation of consensus guidelines.

414
00:59:13,067 --> 00:59:20,405
They highlighted the staggering costs encumbering the healthcare system in the United States as a result of such strategies.

415
00:59:20,405 --> 00:59:32,092
Similar concerns were raised by Kuusela and associates (2021) who evaluated cervical length screening among 11,456 asymptomatic gravidas.

416
00:59:32,092 --> 00:59:34,467
Preterm Birth Prevention.

417
00:59:34,467 --> 00:59:38,280
Prevention of preterm birth remains an elusive goal.

418
00:59:38,280 --> 00:59:44,555
Yet, experts continue to debate the optimal strategies to prevent or manage preterm birth .

419
00:59:44,555 --> 00:59:54,042
Dietary supplementations, for example, have not shown benefit (Makrides, 2019; Palacios, 2019).

420
00:59:54,042 --> 01:00:03,917
Still, recent reports suggest that prevention in selected populations may be achievable (Breslin, 2020; Goodfellow, 2021).

421
01:00:03,917 --> 01:00:06,567
# Cervical Cerclage.

422
01:00:06,567 --> 01:00:12,417
Cerclage placement may be used to prevent preterm birth in at least three circumstances.

423
01:00:12,417 --> 01:00:21,267
First, the procedure may benefit women who have a history of recurrent second-trimester loss and who are diagnosed with cervical insufficiency.

424
01:00:21,267 --> 01:00:27,655
A second instance is the woman identified during sonographic examination to have a short cervix.

425
01:00:27,655 --> 01:00:36,342
The third indication is a “rescue” cerclage, done emergently when cervical incompetence is recognized in women with threatened preterm labor.

426
01:00:36,342 --> 01:00:45,442
Cerclage placement in twin pregnancies for clinical indications is an evolving area (Li, 2019; Roman, 2020).

427
01:00:45,442 --> 01:00:51,630
In this chapter, however, cerclage placement will be in the context of a singleton pregnancy.

428
01:00:51,630 --> 01:00:55,730
An accurate history is critical for management decisions.

429
01:00:55,730 --> 01:01:03,755
For recurrent abortion from cervical incompetence, historical clues are outlined in Chapter 11 (p. 205).

430
01:01:03,755 --> 01:01:14,942
For women with a short cervix incidentally detected by sonography, the benefit of cerclage placement appears directly related to whether the woman has a history of prior preterm birth.

431
01:01:14,942 --> 01:01:22,917
In those without a prior preterm birth, cerclage for a sonographically detected short cervix alone offers no advantage .

432
01:01:22,917 --> 01:01:40,180
To and associates (2004) screened 47,123 women and randomly assigned the 253 women with a cervix measuring <15 mm, with or without a history of preterm birth, to cerclage or no cerclage groups.

433
01:01:40,180 --> 01:01:47,230
The frequency of preterm delivery before 33 weeks did not differ significantly between the two cohorts.

434
01:01:47,230 --> 01:01:54,692
In contrast, women with a sonographically diagnosed short cervix and a history of preterm birth may benefit.

435
01:01:54,692 --> 01:02:07,892
Owen and colleagues (2009) randomly assigned 302 women with prior preterm birth plus a short cervix—defined as length <25 mm—to cerclage or no-procedure groups.

436
01:02:07,892 --> 01:02:12,267
The primary study outcome was not supported by the intervention.

437
01:02:12,267 --> 01:02:25,117
However, women with a cervical length <15 mm delivered before 35 weeks significantly less often following cerclage compared with women with no cerclage—30 versus 65 percent.

438
01:02:25,117 --> 01:02:36,330
This study suggested that recurrent preterm birth could be prevented in a subset of women with asymptomatic singleton gestations with both previous preterm birth and short cervical length.

439
01:02:36,330 --> 01:02:45,705
These findings prompted a reassessment by Berghella and coworkers (2011), who performed a metaanalysis using individual patient data .

440
01:02:45,705 --> 01:02:51,067
The primary outcomes from the included trials did not support cerclage placement.

441
01:02:51,067 --> 01:02:54,755
However, these investigators concluded that

442
01:02:54,916 --> 01:03:09,691
cerclage significantly prevented preterm birth and improved composite perinatal mortality and morbidity outcomes in women with prior spontaneous preterm birth, singleton gestation, and cervical length <25 mm.

443
01:03:09,641 --> 01:03:12,403
Figure 45-5.

444
01:03:12,403 --> 01:03:24,941
Cerclage versus no cerclage for prevention of recurrent preterm birth in women with a cervical length <25 mm. Forest plot analysis of composite perinatal mortality and morbidity.

445
01:03:24,941 --> 01:03:29,828
CI = confidence interval; M-H = Mantel-Haenszel.

446
01:03:29,828 --> 01:03:35,941
One caveat in the interpretation of this cerclage data is the influence of obstetrical history.

447
01:03:35,941 --> 01:03:44,653
For example, all of the trials comprising the metaanalysis included preterm birth as early as 16 to 17 weeks’ gestation.

448
01:03:44,653 --> 01:03:52,441
Defining these early second-trimester losses as preterm births, rather than cervical incompetence, is problematic.

449
01:03:52,441 --> 01:04:01,278
Thus, it is difficult to distinguish whether these women were treated in the context of cervical incompetence or of preterm labor at 16 weeks.

450
01:04:01,278 --> 01:04:25,928
Nonetheless, based on these findings, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021c) concluded that in women with a singleton pregnancy, prior spontaneous preterm birth, cervical length <25 mm, and gestational age <24 weeks, cerclage placement may be considered versus vaginal progesterone (p. 797).

451
01:04:25,928 --> 01:04:32,416
Technique The operative placement of cervical cerclage is shown in Chapter 11 (p. 206).

452
01:04:32,416 --> 01:04:36,403
Several emerging trends, however, merit comment.

453
01:04:36,403 --> 01:04:42,816
For example, indication for cerclage placement may influence selection of suture material .

454
01:04:42,816 --> 01:04:47,991
Tocolysis and antibiotics with cerclage placement is another controversy .

455
01:04:47,991 --> 01:05:02,653
In a retrospective report of 142 cases, 72 received perioperative prophylaxis, which consisted of perioperative cefazolin and indomethacin, during physical examination–indicated cerclage placement.

456
01:05:02,653 --> 01:05:08,366
This practice was associated with a significant prolongation in gestational latency .

457
01:05:08,366 --> 01:05:12,691
At Parkland Hospital, we have not adopted such practices.

458
01:05:12,691 --> 01:05:20,866
Last, for women with a history of a failed cerclage, transabdominal cerclage appears to be superior to vaginal cerclage .

459
01:05:20,866 --> 01:05:24,903
# Prophylaxis with Progestogen Compounds.

460
01:05:24,903 --> 01:05:30,891
In most mammals, progesterone withdrawal is considered to be a parturition-triggering event.

461
01:05:30,891 --> 01:05:38,766
During human parturition, however, maternal, fetal, and amnionic fluid progesterone levels remain elevated.

462
01:05:38,766 --> 01:05:47,653
It has been proposed that human parturition involves functional progesterone withdrawal mediated by decreased activity of progesterone receptors .

463
01:05:47,653 --> 01:05:53,303
It follows conceptually that progesterone administration may prevent preterm labor.

464
01:05:53,303 --> 01:06:04,991
This hypothesis has stimulated several studies of both 17-alpha hydroxyprogesterone caproate and vaginally administered progesterone in women with varying risks for preterm birth.

465
01:06:04,991 --> 01:06:12,366
At present, reported benefits of either progestogen therapy are largely limited to women with singleton pregnancies.

466
01:06:12,366 --> 01:06:26,166
Progestogen prophylaxis specifically in multifetal gestations has not lowered preterm birth rates (Caritis, 2009; Dodd, 2019; Rehal, 2020; Rouse, 2007).

467
01:06:26,166 --> 01:06:45,041
Accordingly, both the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021c) and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020b) support the use of progestogen therapy for prevention of preterm birth in select women with singleton pregnancies despite the challenges chronicled .

468
01:06:45,041 --> 01:06:53,328
Either a history of prior preterm birth or no prior preterm birth but a sonographically identified short cervix is a criterion.

469
01:06:53,328 --> 01:06:57,716
# Prior Preterm Birth and Progestogen Compounds.

470
01:06:57,716 --> 01:07:06,966
The synthetic progestogen 17-alpha-hydroxyprogesterone caproate (17OHPC) is the focus of current controversy.

471
01:07:06,966 --> 01:07:13,866
It remains the first, and only, drug approved by the FDA for prevention of recurrent preterm birth.

472
01:07:13,866 --> 01:07:26,628
This therapy was originally approved in 2011 through an accelerated process for orphan drugs and based on the findings of Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units (MFMU) Network study, described next.

473
01:07:26,628 --> 01:07:41,053
However, this approval and justification for continued use is now disputed (Chang, 2020; Food and Drug Administration, 2019, 2020; Greene, 2020; Nelson, 2021).

474
01:07:41,053 --> 01:07:50,528
Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units Network Trial In the MFMU Network trial, 463 women with a prior preterm birth were randomly

475
01:07:50,692 --> 01:08:00,079
assigned to receive weekly intramuscular injections of inert oil placebo or 17-OHPC from 16 through 36 weeks’ gestation .

476
01:08:00,029 --> 01:08:09,117
Recurrence of preterm birth rates were 36 percent in women receiving 17OHPC and 55 percent in those given placebo.

477
01:08:09,117 --> 01:08:15,442
This study was challenged because of the unexpectedly high preterm delivery rate in the placebo arm .

478
01:08:15,442 --> 01:08:20,642
One explanation for this high rate was asymmetry in the risks of recurrence.

479
01:08:20,642 --> 01:08:30,979
Indeed, 41 percent of the placebo group had ≥2 prior preterm births compared with only 28 percent in the 17-OHPC group.

480
01:08:30,979 --> 01:08:43,154
Another concern was that the injection dosage of 17-OHPC, which was 250 mg weekly, was empirically chosen (Caritis, 2012, 2014).

481
01:08:43,154 --> 01:08:52,617
Because of these reported challenges, the FDA granted approval but called for a confirmatory randomized clinical trial before final FDA approval .

482
01:08:52,617 --> 01:09:00,417
The multicenter, international, randomized trial was launched in 2009 but required 9 years to complete .

483
01:09:00,417 --> 01:09:10,967
Pricing Concerns Following the accelerated approval, drug-overpricing claims prompted concern (Cohen, 2011; Romero, 2013).

484
01:09:10,967 --> 01:09:20,154
In 2011, the FDA gave temporary approval to KV Pharmaceutical to market 17-OHPC under the brand name Makena.

485
01:09:20,154 --> 01:09:31,367
Because regulations prohibited compounding, there was no competitor for this relatively inexpensive drug, and Makena was priced at $1500 per injection.

486
01:09:31,367 --> 01:09:38,767
This caused widespread concern because the cumulative cost of Makena would be more than $30,000 per pregnancy.

487
01:09:38,767 --> 01:09:53,529
Parkland Hospital, given these pricing concerns, contracted a local compounding pharmacy to provide 250-mg, single-dose vials of 17-OHPC in sesame oil at a cost of $25 per dose.

488
01:09:53,529 --> 01:10:04,342
Nelson and colleagues (2017) reported their findings from this program in a prospective study of 430 women given this compounded 17-OHPC.

489
01:10:04,342 --> 01:10:13,167
It was ineffective for prevention of recurrent preterm birth at 35 weeks or less compared with a historical cohort from Parkland Hospital.

490
01:10:13,167 --> 01:10:22,954
Moreover, 17-OHPC did not significantly reduce the rates of recurrent preterm birth regardless of prior preterm birth number or sequence.

491
01:10:22,954 --> 01:10:35,492
Moreover, plasma concentrations of 17-OHPC were not different at 24 weeks or 32 weeks between women delivered at ≤35 weeks and those delivered later.

492
01:10:35,492 --> 01:10:42,954
Metabolism Despite its widespread use, the mechanism of action for 17-OHPC remains unknown.

493
01:10:42,954 --> 01:10:53,604
Sharma and associates (2008) reported that the metabolism of 17OHPC was predominantly mediated by the CYP3A enzymatic system.

494
01:10:53,604 --> 01:11:00,204
Thus, other agents that induce or inhibit this system or hepatic impairment may alter drug levels.

495
01:11:00,204 --> 01:11:09,617
They also showed that 17-OHPC is not converted after administration to the progesterone metabolite 17α-hydroxyprogesterone.

496
01:11:09,617 --> 01:11:18,167
The relative binding affinity of 17-OHPC to progesterone receptors approximates only 30 percent of that by progesterone .

497
01:11:18,167 --> 01:11:30,267
Caritis and colleagues (2012) examined 61 women receiving 17-OHPC therapy and found that the half-life was relatively long (median 16.2 days).

498
01:11:30,267 --> 01:11:37,154
Pharmacokinetic parameters were affected by maternal body habitus and varied widely between subjects.

499
01:11:37,154 --> 01:11:46,554
In addition, 17-OHPC crossed the placental barrier and was detectible in cord plasma 44 days after the last maternal injection .

500
01:11:46,554 --> 01:11:52,692
Despite this, evidence to date suggests that 17OHPC is safe for the fetus .

501
01:11:52,692 --> 01:12:03,517
No abnormalities, including abnormal genitalia, were found in a 48-month follow-up study of infants exposed in the 2003 MFMU Network trial .

502
01:12:03,517 --> 01:12:14,554
Simons and coworkers (2021) also reported no effect of progestogens on child development in a systematic review comprising numerous developmental measurements.

503
01:12:14,554 --> 01:12:22,254
PROLONG Trial The FDA-required confirmatory trial was the PROLONG trial and completed in October 2018.

504
01:12:22,254 --> 01:12:30,367
A total of 1708 women were randomly assigned in a 2:1 ratio to receive 17-OHPC or placebo.

505
01:12:30,367 --> 01:12:38,154
This trial had two primary endpoints, which were birth <35 weeks’ gestation and a neonatal composite.

506
01:12:38,154 --> 01:12:42,867
Data were analyzed for 1651 of the liveborn neonates .

507
01:12:42,867 --> 01:12:47,542
Progesterone was not effective in preventing recurrent preterm birth.

508
01:12:47,542 --> 01:12:54,692
In the analysis of various secondary outcomes, 17-OHPC treatment also lacked efficacy.

509
01:12:54,692 --> 01:12:57,392
Table 45-7.

510
01:12:57,392 --> 01:13:05,217
Perinatal Outcomes of PROLONG Trial FDA Recommendations In October 2019, the FDA convened an

511
01:13:05,380 --> 01:13:10,630
Advisory Committee meeting to review 17-OHPC and the new data.

512
01:13:10,580 --> 01:13:18,917
Efficacy, side effects, and several subgroup analyses were studied, and none supported 17-OHPC efficacy .

513
01:13:18,917 --> 01:13:32,667
The FDA concluded that the PROLONG trial did not demonstrate a treatment benefit of 17-OHPC in reducing the neonatal composite index or the rate of spontaneous preterm birth prior to 35 weeks’ gestation.

514
01:13:32,667 --> 01:13:36,505
The Advisory Committee recommended to withdraw approval.

515
01:13:36,505 --> 01:13:46,155
In November 2020, the Center for Drug Evaluation and Research proposed that Makena (17-OHPC) be withdrawn from the market .

516
01:13:46,155 --> 01:13:48,905
Figure 45-6.

517
01:13:48,905 --> 01:14:01,217
In analysis of co-primary endpoints for the PROLONG Trial, five of the included defining risk factors were noted to be different from those in the Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units Network trial (see text).

518
01:14:01,217 --> 01:14:12,855
These are black race, >1 prior spontaneous preterm birth, single or without a partner, substance use during pregnancy, and ≤12 years of formal education.

519
01:14:12,855 --> 01:14:25,605
The three groups examined in this post-hoc composite risk profile analysis were those with no risk factors, those with ≥1 risk factor, and those with ≥2 risk factors.

520
01:14:25,605 --> 01:14:29,580
Makena is the brand name of 17-OHPC.

521
01:14:29,580 --> 01:14:33,217
Response to the FDA position has been mixed.

522
01:14:33,217 --> 01:14:51,780
Both the American College of Obstetricians (2019a, 2021a) and Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020b) have issued statements endorsing continued use of 17OHPC for prevention of recurrent preterm birth in women with singleton pregnancies.

523
01:14:51,780 --> 01:15:04,667
Endorsements by these organizations continue after publication of a metaanalysis by the Evaluating Progestogens for Preventing Preterm birth International Collaborative (EPPPIC) Group (2021).

524
01:15:04,667 --> 01:15:15,655
These investigators summarized that vaginal progesterone and 17-OHPC both reduced birth rates before 34 weeks’ gestation in high-risk singleton pregnancies.

525
01:15:15,655 --> 01:15:21,842
Notably, the findings for 17-OHPC did not reach statistical significance.

526
01:15:21,842 --> 01:15:28,205
Moreover, this metaanalysis grouped together trials of patients with differing risk profiles.

527
01:15:28,205 --> 01:15:34,830
It combined women with and without a prior preterm birth as well as women with and without a short cervix.

528
01:15:34,830 --> 01:15:46,880
These positions have stimulated several dueling commentaries regarding its use (Chang, 2020; Godlewski, 2020; Greene, 2020; Sibai, 2020).

529
01:15:46,880 --> 01:15:51,755
At this time, the FDA has not withdrawn the accelerated approval.

530
01:15:51,755 --> 01:16:01,092
If formally withdrawn, however, a consequence may be continued “off-label” use, with the caveat that insurers may not provide payment coverage.

531
01:16:01,092 --> 01:16:05,405
# Progesterone Prophylaxis with Shortened Cervix.

532
01:16:05,405 --> 01:16:14,492
Three randomized trials are at the center of whether progestogen therapy should be used in women without prior preterm births but who have a shortened cervix .

533
01:16:14,492 --> 01:16:32,242
In the first trial, 250 women with short cervices measuring ≤15 mm identified during routine prenatal care were nightly given 200-mg micronized progesterone vaginal capsules or placebo from 24 to 34 weeks’ gestation .

534
01:16:32,242 --> 01:16:39,180
The rate of spontaneous delivery <34 weeks was significantly reduced by progesterone therapy.

535
01:16:39,180 --> 01:16:45,892
Importantly, this trial included nulliparas and also those with twins or prior preterm birth.

536
01:16:45,892 --> 01:16:48,455
Table 45-8.

537
01:16:48,455 --> 01:17:05,617
Randomized Trials of Progestogen Compounds Given Prophylactically to Prevent Preterm Labor In the second trial, 465 women with a short cervix—10 to 20 mm—were given vaginal progesterone gel, 90 mg daily, or placebo .

538
01:17:05,617 --> 01:17:12,105
Those receiving progesterone had significantly lower rates of preterm birth <33 weeks.

539
01:17:12,105 --> 01:17:17,205
This trial also included nulliparas and women with prior preterm births.

540
01:17:17,205 --> 01:17:37,230
According to Likis and colleagues (2012), the heterogeneity of these first two studies that included women with varied indications for progestogen treatment, combined with the fact that outcomes were not reported by risk factors such as nulliparity, made it impossible to interpret the efficacy of progesterone for specific indications.

541
01:17:37,230 --> 01:17:54,392
The third study randomly assigned administration of 17-OHPC intramuscular injection or placebo between 16 and 223/7 weeks’ gestation to nulliparas with a singleton gestation and a cervical length <30 mm detected sonographically .

542
01:17:54,392 --> 01:18:01,417
Treatment with 17-OHPC did not reduce the frequency of preterm birth <37 weeks.

543
01:18:01,417 --> 01:18:03,317
Regardless of

544
01:18:03,484 --> 01:18:07,871
cervical length, 17OHPC was ineffective.

545
01:18:07,821 --> 01:18:17,646
From these, vaginal progesterone, but not intramuscular 17-OHPC, appears to benefit women with a sonographically measured short cervix.

546
01:18:17,646 --> 01:18:32,409
Romero and Stanczyk (2013) provided a review to explain the conflicting evidence and argued that naturally occurring progesterone, which is used in the vaginal preparations, is not the same as synthetic 17-OHPC.

547
01:18:32,409 --> 01:18:44,046
Likewise, Furcron and coworkers (2015) found that 17-OHPC did not have local antiinflammatory effects at the maternal-fetal interface or cervix.

548
01:18:44,046 --> 01:18:50,409
Further, 17OHPC did not protect against endotoxin-induced preterm birth.

549
01:18:50,409 --> 01:19:03,546
From all these studies, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021c) concluded that universal cervical length screening in women without a prior preterm birth is not mandatory.

550
01:19:03,546 --> 01:19:10,259
However, this screening strategy could be considered in the context of treatment with vaginal progesterone.

551
01:19:10,259 --> 01:19:22,646
Because of the preliminary results suggesting success of vaginally administered progesterone, the OPPTIMUM study was carried out—does progesterone prophylaxis to prevent preterm labor improve outcome?

552
01:19:22,646 --> 01:19:38,896
High-risk women were defined as those with a prior spontaneous birth ≤34 weeks or with a cervical length ≤25 mm or a positive fFN test result combined with other clinical risk factors for preterm birth.

553
01:19:38,896 --> 01:19:46,021
The primary outcomes of OPPTIMUM were unique in that both immediate obstetrical and childhood outcomes were examined.

554
01:19:46,021 --> 01:19:57,646
These were fetal death or birth <34 weeks; a composite of death, brain injury, or bronchopulmonary dysplasia; and a standardized cognitive score at age 2 years.

555
01:19:57,646 --> 01:20:07,284
Contrary to earlier reports, vaginal progesterone was not associated with a lower risk of preterm birth or composite neonatal adverse outcomes.

556
01:20:07,284 --> 01:20:13,196
In children at 2 years, vaginal progesterone had no long-term benefit or harm.

557
01:20:13,196 --> 01:20:15,984
# Progestogens Summary.

558
01:20:15,984 --> 01:20:22,821
Thus, evidence remains conflicting as to the efficacy of progestogens across various indications.

559
01:20:22,821 --> 01:20:38,521
Some have attempted to resolve these issues through systematic review and metaanalysis (Conde-Agudelo, 2018; da Fonseca, 2020; Jarde, 2019; Prior, 2017; Romero, 2018).

560
01:20:38,521 --> 01:20:46,096
However, virtually all evidence supporting progestogen use for a specific indication can be challenged in some way.

561
01:20:46,096 --> 01:21:06,559
At this time, we agree with Norman and colleagues (2016) that the results of recent studies should prompt a major review of progesterone use for preterm birth prophylaxis, a search to identify specific women who might benefit, and a redoubling of efforts to find alternative strategies to prevent preterm birth in women at risk.

562
01:21:06,559 --> 01:21:10,459
# Geography-based Public Health-care Programs.

563
01:21:10,459 --> 01:21:17,159
A well-organized prenatal system can lower preterm birth rates in high-risk indigent populations .

564
01:21:17,159 --> 01:21:29,734
For example, in the Parkland Hospital prenatal clinic system, a declining preterm birth rate between 1988 and 2006 coincided with a substantial rise in prenatal visit attendance .

565
01:21:29,734 --> 01:21:41,071
In the early 1990s, a concerted effort was made to improve access by creating seamless care that began with antenatal enrollment and extended through delivery and the puerperium.

566
01:21:41,071 --> 01:21:47,971
Prenatal clinics were placed strategically throughout Dallas County to provide convenient access for patients.

567
01:21:47,971 --> 01:21:54,821
Prenatal protocols are used by nurse practitioners at all clinic sites to guarantee homogeneous care.

568
01:21:54,821 --> 01:22:01,171
Women with high-risk pregnancy complications are referred to a hospital-based central clinic system.

569
01:22:01,171 --> 01:22:10,109
Here, maternal-fetal medicine clinics operate each weekday and are staffed by residents and midwives who are supervised by fellows and faculty.

570
01:22:10,109 --> 01:22:12,809
Figure 45-7.

571
01:22:12,809 --> 01:22:24,346
Percentage of births before 37 weeks’ gestation at Parkland Hospital from 1988 to 2006 compared with that in the United States from 1996 to 2002.

572
01:22:24,346 --> 01:22:32,996
Analysis in both cohorts was limited to singleton liveborn infants ≥500 g who received prenatal care.

573
01:22:32,996 --> 01:22:43,059
Reproduced with permission from Leveno KJ, McIntire DD, Bloom SL, et al: Decreased preterm births in an inner-city public hospital.

574
01:22:43,059 --> 01:22:50,221
Obstet Gynecol. 2009 Mar;113(3):578–584.

575
01:22:50,221 --> 01:22:58,321
A similar obstetrical care system for indigent women at the University of Alabama at Birmingham also has produced salutary

576
01:22:58,492 --> 01:23:00,129
results .

577
01:23:00,079 --> 01:23:08,242
Given the recent emphasis on access to care, especially among underserved minorities, these experiences remain relevant .

578
01:23:08,242 --> 01:23:16,379
Prenatal care is an important component of a comprehensive public health-care system that at least partially affects the preterm birth rate.

579
01:23:16,379 --> 01:23:18,529
# Aspirin.

580
01:23:18,529 --> 01:23:31,929
Acetylsalicylic acid (ASA) is a cyclooxygenase inhibitor with antiinflammatory and antiplatelet properties that has been used in pregnancy most commonly to prevent or delay onset of preeclampsia .

581
01:23:31,929 --> 01:23:38,142
Data now suggest that low-dose aspirin may also reduce spontaneous preterm birth rates .

582
01:23:38,142 --> 01:23:56,067
In one randomized study of 11,976 nulliparas in six low- and middle-income countries, women were randomly assigned to receive low-dose aspirin (81 mg) or placebo initiated between 60/7 and 136/7 weeks’ gestation .

583
01:23:56,067 --> 01:24:03,529
Low-dose aspirin decreased the incidence of birth <37 weeks and reduced the perinatal mortality rate.

584
01:24:03,529 --> 01:24:12,092
Although this trial did not differentiate spontaneous from indicated preterm birth, it requires replication in high-resource settings.

585
01:24:12,092 --> 01:24:25,429
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020c) does not recommend low-dose aspirin prophylaxis to prevent spontaneous preterm birth in the absence of preeclampsia risk factors.

586
01:24:25,429 --> 01:24:28,804
Management Of Preterm Prelabor Rupture.

587
01:24:28,804 --> 01:24:30,667
Of Membranes.

588
01:24:30,667 --> 01:24:37,529
Methods used to diagnose ruptured membranes are detailed in Chapter 22 (p. 426).

589
01:24:37,529 --> 01:24:51,404
A history of vaginal leakage of fluid, either as a continuous stream or a gush, should prompt a speculum examination to visualize gross vaginal pooling of amnionic fluid, clear fluid from the cervical canal, or both.

590
01:24:51,404 --> 01:25:05,479
Confirmation of rupture of membranes is usually accompanied by sonographic examination to assess amnionic fluid volume, to identify the presenting part, and if not previously determined, to estimate gestational age.

591
01:25:05,479 --> 01:25:12,892
Once PPROM is identified, the general scheme shown in Table 45-9 can guide management.

592
01:25:12,892 --> 01:25:15,492
Table 45-9.

593
01:25:15,492 --> 01:25:20,392
Management of Prelabor Rupture of Membranes # Natural History.

594
01:25:20,392 --> 01:25:27,354
The time from PPROM to delivery is inversely related to the gestational age at which rupture occurs.

595
01:25:27,354 --> 01:25:33,742
Very few days are gained if membranes rupture during the third trimester compared with midpregnancy .

596
01:25:33,742 --> 01:25:36,292
Figure 45-8.

597
01:25:36,292 --> 01:25:43,942
Relationship of time between preterm membrane rupture and delivery in 172 singleton pregnancies.

598
01:25:43,942 --> 01:25:53,167
Reproduced with permission from Carroll SG, Blott M, Nicolaides KH: Preterm prelabor amniorrhexis: Outcome of live births.

599
01:25:53,167 --> 01:25:59,304
Obstet Gynecol 1995 Jul;86(1):18–25.

600
01:25:59,304 --> 01:26:10,754
In an earlier study from Parkland Hospital, 298 consecutive women with spontaneously ruptured membranes between 24 and 34 weeks’ gestation were analyzed .

601
01:26:10,754 --> 01:26:15,967
PPROM constituted 1.7 percent of pregnancies during the study period.

602
01:26:15,967 --> 01:26:24,467
At the time they presented, 76 percent of the women were already in labor, and 5 percent were delivered for other complications.

603
01:26:24,467 --> 01:26:29,742
Thus, only 19 percent initially were suitable for expectant management.

604
01:26:29,742 --> 01:26:38,117
Ultimately, delivery was delayed 48 hours or more after membrane rupture in only 7 percent of the total study cohort.

605
01:26:38,117 --> 01:26:41,004
None of the neonates in this group died.

606
01:26:41,004 --> 01:26:49,017
This contrasted with a neonatal death rate of 8 percent in preterm newborns delivered within 48 hours of membrane rupture.

607
01:26:49,017 --> 01:26:51,929
Others have reported similar results .

608
01:26:51,929 --> 01:26:54,504
# Hospitalization.

609
01:26:54,504 --> 01:26:58,692
Most clinicians hospitalize women with PPROM.

610
01:26:58,692 --> 01:27:07,979
Concerns regarding the costs of lengthy hospitalizations are usually moot, because most women enter labor within a week or less after membrane rupture.

611
01:27:07,979 --> 01:27:18,792
Carlan and coworkers (1993) randomly assigned 67 women with PPROM at <37 weeks’ gestation to home or hospital management.

612
01:27:18,792 --> 01:27:22,367
The mean gestational age was 31 weeks.

613
01:27:22,367 --> 01:27:32,129
No benefits were found for hospitalization, and maternal hospital stays were reduced by 50 percent in those sent home—14 versus 7 days.

614
01:27:32,129 --> 01:27:41,517
Importantly, the investigators emphasized that this study was too small to conclude that home management was safe in regard to umbilical cord prolapse.

615
01:27:41,517 --> 01:27:48,142
If hospitalization is chosen, no consensus guides the optimal frequency of inpatient assessment.

616
01:27:48,142 --> 01:27:58,642
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020g) notes that an acceptable strategy would include periodic sonograms for fetal growth,

617
01:27:58,804 --> 01:28:05,241
fetal heart rate monitoring, and clinical surveillance for maternal infection—particularly fever.

618
01:28:05,191 --> 01:28:17,691
During the periviable period and at gestation ages before interventions would be planned, a short period of initial observation and consideration of outpatient monitoring in select cases is reasonable.

619
01:28:17,691 --> 01:28:21,929
Hospitalization would then be practiced at the time of viability.

620
01:28:21,929 --> 01:28:24,641
# Intentional Delivery.

621
01:28:24,641 --> 01:28:31,941
Before the mid-1970s, labor was usually induced in women with PPROM because of sepsis fears.

622
01:28:31,941 --> 01:28:41,879
Maternal infection risk and fetal prematurity risk vary according to the gestational age at membrane rupture, and management decisions incorporate this data.

623
01:28:41,879 --> 01:28:52,554
With periviable pregnancy, Morales (1993b) expectantly managed 94 singleton pregnancies with PPROM before 25 weeks.

624
01:28:52,554 --> 01:28:55,754
The average time gained was 11 days.

625
01:28:55,754 --> 01:29:04,279
Although 41 percent of infants survived to age 1 year, only 27 percent of the original cohort were neurologically normal.

626
01:29:04,279 --> 01:29:11,604
Similar results were reported by Farooqi (1998) and Winn (2000) and their colleagues.

627
01:29:11,604 --> 01:29:17,041
Management of these early pregnancies is discussed on page 785.

628
01:29:17,041 --> 01:29:29,041
For PPROM in general, two randomized trials in the 1990s compared labor induction with expectant management (Cox, 1995; Mercer, 1993).

629
01:29:29,041 --> 01:29:39,941
In both of these studies, the balance of risk and benefit was difficult to ascertain, as neither immediate delivery nor expectant management was superior for neonatal outcomes.

630
01:29:39,941 --> 01:29:48,879
Lieman and associates (2005) found that neonatal outcomes did not improve with expectant management beyond 33 weeks.

631
01:29:48,879 --> 01:29:59,441
McElrath and coworkers (2003) found that prolonged latency after membrane rupture was not associated with a greater incidence of fetal neurological damage.

632
01:29:59,441 --> 01:30:11,666
An important correlate is that infection—specifically chorioamnionitis—is a recognized risk factor for neonatal neurological injury (Gaudet, 2001; Wu, 2000).

633
01:30:11,666 --> 01:30:21,566
Bond and colleagues (2017) compared planned early birth with expectant management for women with PPROM before 37 weeks’ gestation.

634
01:30:21,566 --> 01:30:30,416
They evaluated 12 randomized trials totaling 3617 women and 3628 newborns.

635
01:30:30,416 --> 01:30:39,741
No clinically important differences in the incidence of neonatal sepsis between women who immediately delivered and those managed expectantly were identified.

636
01:30:39,741 --> 01:30:51,566
Although the incidence of chorioamnionitis was lower, neonates of women randomized to early birth were more likely to be born at an earlier gestational age and encountered attendant perinatal sequelae.

637
01:30:51,566 --> 01:31:07,466
The authors concluded that in women with rupture of membranes before 37 weeks’ gestation without contraindications to continuing the pregnancy, a policy of expectant management with careful monitoring was associated with better outcomes for both the mother and newborn.

638
01:31:07,466 --> 01:31:22,916
A subsequent metaanalysis of late-preterm prelabor rupture of membranes by Quist-Nelson and coworkers (2018) noted that immediate delivery and expectant management resulted in comparable rates of the composite of adverse neonatal outcomes.

639
01:31:22,916 --> 01:31:36,241
Because of conflicting data and the controversies of immediate delivery compared with expectant management, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020g) cannot make firm recommendations.

640
01:31:36,241 --> 01:31:40,516
Clearly, gestational age is an important consideration.

641
01:31:40,516 --> 01:31:53,129
At 240/7 to 336/7 weeks, expectant management in the absence of nonreassuring fetal status, clinical chorioamnionitis, or placental abruption is recommended.

642
01:31:53,129 --> 01:32:10,766
At 340/7 to 366/7 weeks’ gestation, delivery was once recommended, however, it is now considered reasonable to offer either expectant management or immediate delivery following consideration of risks and benefits, counseling, and shared decision-making.

643
01:32:10,766 --> 01:32:15,954
Expectant management should not go beyond 370/7 weeks’ gestation.

644
01:32:15,954 --> 01:32:26,754
Based upon limitations of access to care and resources, our current practices at Parkland Hospital continue to favor immediate delivery after 34 weeks’ gestation.

645
01:32:26,754 --> 01:32:29,479
# Expectant Management.

646
01:32:29,479 --> 01:32:38,141
Several considerations arise during expectant management of PPROM (Boettcher, 2020; Shaddeau, 2020).

647
01:32:38,141 --> 01:32:41,216
One is digital cervical examination.

648
01:32:41,216 --> 01:32:50,754
Alexander and colleagues (2000) analyzed findings in women with membrane rupture expectantly managed between 24 and 32 weeks’

649
01:32:50,908 --> 01:32:52,645
gestation.

650
01:32:52,595 --> 01:32:58,845
They compared those who had one or two digital cervical examinations with women who were not examined.

651
01:32:58,845 --> 01:33:05,833
Those who were examined had a rupture-todelivery interval of 3 days compared with 5 days in those not examined.

652
01:33:05,833 --> 01:33:10,208
This difference did not worsen maternal or neonatal outcomes.

653
01:33:10,208 --> 01:33:17,295
At Parkland Hospital, digital cervical examination is used judiciously in PPROM cases.

654
01:33:17,295 --> 01:33:22,620
Rupture of membranes following second-trimester amniocentesis is uncommon .

655
01:33:22,620 --> 01:33:38,083
Compared with women with spontaneous rupture during the second trimester, Borgida and associates (2000) found that pregnancies complicated by PPROM after genetic amniocentesis resulted in significantly better perinatal outcomes.

656
01:33:38,083 --> 01:33:41,870
The perinatal survival rate was 91 percent.

657
01:33:41,870 --> 01:33:52,170
After counseling, affected women are typically managed expectantly as outpatients with serial surveillance of amnionic fluid volume and selfassessment of temperature .

658
01:33:52,170 --> 01:34:01,545
In the series cited above, the mean time to documentation of a normal amnionic fluid volume after amniocentesis approximated 2 weeks.

659
01:34:01,545 --> 01:34:06,458
Tocolysis given with PPROM has been reported in few studies.

660
01:34:06,458 --> 01:34:16,933
In women with ruptured membranes and lack of labor, prophylactic tocolysis does not improve neonatal outcomes but is associated with greater chorioamnionitis rates .

661
01:34:16,933 --> 01:34:25,533
Similarly, therapeutic tocolysis— for those with ruptured membranes and labor—has not provided significant perinatal benefit .

662
01:34:25,533 --> 01:34:31,270
Management of PPROM in the woman who has undergone cervical cerclage is complex .

663
01:34:31,270 --> 01:34:41,145
McElrath and associates (2002) studied 114 women with a cerclage in place who later had ruptured membranes before 34 weeks.

664
01:34:41,145 --> 01:34:44,708
They were compared with 288 controls.

665
01:34:44,708 --> 01:34:48,445
Pregnancy outcomes were equivalent in both groups.

666
01:34:48,445 --> 01:35:04,933
Cerclage retention for more than 24 hours after PPROM may be associated with pregnancy prolongation, however, intrauterine infection and its consequences are risks (Giraldo-Isaza, 2011; Laskin, 2012).

667
01:35:04,933 --> 01:35:14,933
At Parkland Hospital, cerclage retention in the absence of infection or labor is currently practiced with close clinical surveillance in those with PPROM.

668
01:35:14,933 --> 01:35:28,208
With PPROM in pregnancies before 26 weeks, the volume of amnionic fluid remaining after rupture appears to have prognostic importance (Carroll, 1995; Hadi, 1994).

669
01:35:28,208 --> 01:35:38,558
Although varied by report, current rates of pulmonary hypoplasia with PPROM before 24 weeks among surviving neonates can be as high as 30 percent .

670
01:35:38,558 --> 01:35:44,333
This suggests that 23 weeks reflects a threshold for lung hypoplasia development .

671
01:35:44,333 --> 01:35:53,983
Further, when contemplating early expectant management, oligohydramnios and resultant limb compression deformities are influencing considerations.

672
01:35:53,983 --> 01:36:05,208
For neonates born to women who have active herpetic lesions and who are expectantly managed, the infectious morbidity risk appears to be outweighed by risks associated with preterm birth .

673
01:36:05,208 --> 01:36:19,233
Lewis and associates (2007) found that expectant management of women with PPROM and noncephalic presentation was associated with a higher rate of umbilical cord prolapse, especially before 26 weeks.

674
01:36:19,233 --> 01:36:23,658
Tissue sealants are often used to gain surgical hemostasis.

675
01:36:23,658 --> 01:36:28,270
Limited reports describe their use for repair of fetal membranes .

676
01:36:28,270 --> 01:36:38,258
Crowley and coworkers (2016) concluded that available data are currently insufficient to assess the value of sealing procedures for PPROM.

677
01:36:38,258 --> 01:36:41,708
We do not use these agents for this indication.

678
01:36:41,708 --> 01:36:44,933
# Clinical Chorioamnionitis.

679
01:36:44,933 --> 01:36:48,695
Infection is a major concern with membrane rupture.

680
01:36:48,695 --> 01:36:58,895
Although some cases remain subclinical, if chorioamnionitis is diagnosed, prompt efforts to effect delivery, preferably vaginally, are initiated.

681
01:36:58,895 --> 01:37:08,408
Because maternal leukocytosis alone is not a consistent finding, fever is the only reliable indicator for the diagnosis of chorioamnionitis.

682
01:37:08,408 --> 01:37:13,858
Institutional practices and protocols vary in defining the temperature threshold.

683
01:37:13,858 --> 01:37:24,770
Traditionally, a temperature ≥38°C (100.4°F) accompanying ruptured membranes has implied infection.

684
01:37:24,770 --> 01:37:29,008
At Parkland Hospital, we still adhere to this criterion.

685
01:37:29,008 --> 01:37:39,220
In 2015, a workshop sponsored by the NICHD was convened and suggested renaming this condition intraamnionic infection and inflammation —triple I .

686
01:37:39,220 --> 01:37:42,770
The merits of this terminology have been questioned .

687
01:37:42,940 --> 01:37:54,177
Nonetheless, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) revised both the definitions and temperature thresholds for intraamnionic infection.

688
01:37:54,127 --> 01:38:13,302
Using these new definitions, the diagnosis of suspected intraamnionic infection is made when the maternal temperature is ≥39.0°C or when the maternal temperature is 38.0 to 38.9°C and one additional clinical risk factor is present.

689
01:38:13,302 --> 01:38:30,365
The latter include low parity, multiple digital examinations, internal uterine and fetal monitors, meconium-stained amnionic fluid, and the presence of certain genital tract pathogens, such as group B streptococcus (GBS) and sexually transmitted agents.

690
01:38:30,365 --> 01:38:44,827
Isolated maternal fever is defined as any maternal temperature between 38.0°C and 38.9°C with no additional risk factors present, and with or without persistent temperature elevation.

691
01:38:44,827 --> 01:38:50,752
With chorioamnionitis, fetal and neonatal morbidity are substantively increased.

692
01:38:50,752 --> 01:38:59,415
Alexander and colleagues (1998) studied 1367 very-lowbirthweight neonates delivered at Parkland Hospital.

693
01:38:59,415 --> 01:39:08,915
Approximately 7 percent were born to women with overt chorioamnionitis, and their outcomes were compared with similar newborns without clinical infection.

694
01:39:08,915 --> 01:39:19,902
Those in the infected group had higher incidences of sepsis, respiratory distress, early-onset seizures, intraventricular hemorrhage, and periventricular leukomalacia.

695
01:39:19,902 --> 01:39:28,990
Yoon and colleagues (2000) found that intraamnionic infection in preterm neonates was associated with increased rates of cerebral palsy.

696
01:39:28,990 --> 01:39:40,502
In one study of more than 11 million singleton live births in the United States from 1995 to 1997, 1.6 percent of gravidas had fever during labor.

697
01:39:40,502 --> 01:39:46,315
This was a strong predictor of infection-related death in both term and preterm neonates .

698
01:39:46,315 --> 01:39:49,377
# Antimicrobial Therapy.

699
01:39:49,377 --> 01:39:54,727
Various antimicrobials have been evaluated to forestall preterm delivery.

700
01:39:54,727 --> 01:40:04,940
Mercer and associates (1995) reviewed 13 randomized trials that evaluated antibiotics for PPROM before 35 weeks.

701
01:40:04,940 --> 01:40:22,015
Their metaanalysis indicated that only three of 10 outcomes were possibly benefited: (1) fewer women developed chorioamnionitis, (2) fewer newborns developed sepsis, and (3) pregnancy was more often prolonged 7 days in women given antibiotics.

702
01:40:22,015 --> 01:40:29,402
However, rates of other neonatal outcomes, including survival and respiratory distress, were unaffected.

703
01:40:29,402 --> 01:40:42,552
In an MFMU Network trial, women with membrane rupture between 24 and 32 weeks’ gestation were randomly assigned to expectant management combined with placebo or to a 7-day antibiotic regimen.

704
01:40:42,552 --> 01:40:54,865
Treatment included intravenous ampicillin plus erythromycin every 6 hours for 48 hours, which was followed by oral amoxicillin plus erythromycin, every 8 hours for 5 days.

705
01:40:54,865 --> 01:40:58,652
Neither tocolytics nor corticosteroids were given.

706
01:40:58,652 --> 01:41:08,615
Antimicrobialtreated women had significantly fewer newborns with respiratory distress, necrotizing enterocolitis, and composite adverse outcomes .

707
01:41:08,615 --> 01:41:12,440
The latency period was also significantly longer.

708
01:41:12,440 --> 01:41:23,390
Specifically, 50 percent of women given an antimicrobial regimen remained undelivered after 7 days of treatment compared with only 25 percent of those given placebo.

709
01:41:23,390 --> 01:41:30,290
Also, a significantly greater number of treated pregnancies were undelivered at 14 and 21 days.

710
01:41:30,290 --> 01:41:35,127
Cervicovaginal GBS colonization did not alter these results.

711
01:41:35,127 --> 01:41:41,965
Other studies have examined the efficacy of shorter treatment lengths and different antimicrobial combinations.

712
01:41:41,965 --> 01:41:55,152
Three-day treatments compared with 7day regimens using either ampicillin or ampicillin-sulbactam appear equally effective in regard to perinatal outcomes (Lewis, 2003; Segel, 2003).

713
01:41:55,152 --> 01:42:02,065
Similarly, erythromycin compared with placebo offered a range of significant neonatal benefits.

714
01:42:02,065 --> 01:42:12,552
The substitution of azithromycin for erythromycin does not appear to impact maternal or neonatal outcomes, and the former has an improved side-effect profile .

715
01:42:12,552 --> 01:42:22,890
An amoxicillinclavulanate regimen is not recommended, however, because of its association with an increased incidence of neonatal necrotizing enterocolitis .

716
01:42:22,890 --> 01:42:33,840
Some predicted that prolonged antimicrobial therapy in such pregnancies might have unwanted consequences (Carroll, 1996; Mercer, 1999).

717
01:42:33,840 --> 01:42:37,090
Stoll and associates (2002)

718
01:42:37,252 --> 01:42:46,314
studied 4337 neonates weighing from 400 to 1500 g and born from 1998 to 2000.

719
01:42:46,264 --> 01:42:58,514
Their outcomes were compared with those of 7606 neonates of similar birthweight born from 1991 to 1993 and prior to the practice of antibiotic prophylaxis.

720
01:42:58,514 --> 01:43:03,864
The overall rate of early-onset sepsis did not change between these two epochs.

721
01:43:03,864 --> 01:43:13,952
But, the rate of GBS sepsis dropped from 5.9 per 1000 births in the earlier group to 1.7 per 1000 births in the later group.

722
01:43:13,952 --> 01:43:23,077
Comparing these same epochs, the rate of Escherichia coli sepsis, however, rose from 3.2 to 6.8 per 1000 births.

723
01:43:23,077 --> 01:43:29,114
Almost 85 percent of isolates from the more recent cohort were resistant to ampicillin.

724
01:43:29,114 --> 01:43:35,927
Neonates with early-onset sepsis were more likely to die, especially if they were infected with coliforms.

725
01:43:35,927 --> 01:43:47,439
Long term, Kenyon and coworkers (2008a) found that antimicrobials given for women with PPROM had no effect on the health of children at age 7 years.

726
01:43:47,439 --> 01:43:50,427
# Corticosteroid Therapy.

727
01:43:50,427 --> 01:43:59,802
A single course of corticosteroids is recommended for pregnant women with PPROM between 240/7 and 340/7 weeks’ gestation .

728
01:43:59,802 --> 01:44:08,527
A single course of corticosteroids as early as 230/7 weeks in those at risk for preterm delivery within 7 days may be considered.

729
01:44:08,527 --> 01:44:20,477
A similar controversy is found at the other end of the gestational age spectrum, 340/7 to 366/7 weeks’ gestation and with rescue therapy (p. 803).

730
01:44:20,477 --> 01:44:25,852
These issues were summarized by Battarbee (2020) in a recent review.

731
01:44:25,852 --> 01:44:29,177
Management Of Preterm Labor With Intact.

732
01:44:29,177 --> 01:44:30,964
Membranes.

733
01:44:30,964 --> 01:44:38,589
Women with signs and symptoms of preterm labor with intact membranes are managed similarly to those with PPROM.

734
01:44:38,589 --> 01:44:43,514
If possible, delivery before 34 weeks’ gestation is delayed.

735
01:44:43,514 --> 01:44:49,339
Drugs used to abate or suppress preterm uterine contractions are subsequently discussed.

736
01:44:49,339 --> 01:44:53,102
# Amniocentesis to Detect Infection.

737
01:44:53,102 --> 01:45:01,027
One of every 10 women with preterm labor and intact membranes will have intraamnionic infection that is largely subclinical .

738
01:45:01,027 --> 01:45:12,389
Because of this, several tests have been used to diagnose intraamnionic infection (Andrews, 1995; Romero, 1993; Yoon, 1996).

739
01:45:12,389 --> 01:45:19,964
Although such infection can be identified with a positive test result, routine amniocentesis offers little value.

740
01:45:19,964 --> 01:45:22,952
# Corticosteroid Therapy.

741
01:45:22,952 --> 01:45:35,152
Glucocorticosteroids were found to accelerate lung maturation in preterm sheep fetuses, and Liggins and Howie (1972) evaluated them to treat women with preterm labor.

742
01:45:35,152 --> 01:45:48,489
Corticosteroid therapy was effective in lowering the incidence of respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) and neonatal mortality rates if birth was delayed for at least 24 hours after initiation of betamethasone.

743
01:45:48,489 --> 01:45:56,152
Infants exposed to corticosteroids in these early studies have now been followed into adulthood with no ill effects detected.

744
01:45:56,152 --> 01:46:08,014
In 1995, a National Institutes of Health (NIH) Consensus Development Conference panel recommended corticosteroids for fetal lung maturation in threatened preterm birth.

745
01:46:08,014 --> 01:46:24,214
A subsequent NIH Conference (2000) summarized that data were insufficient to assess corticosteroid effectiveness in pregnancies complicated by hypertension, diabetes, multifetal gestation, fetal-growth restriction, or fetal hydrops.

746
01:46:24,214 --> 01:46:30,289
It was concluded, however, that administering corticosteroids to these women is reasonable.

747
01:46:30,289 --> 01:46:42,364
One metaanalysis of 30 studies totaling 7774 women and 8158 infants quantified the benefit of a single course of corticosteroids .

748
01:46:42,364 --> 01:46:57,152
Treatment was associated with lower rates of perinatal death, neonatal death, RDS, intraventricular hemorrhage, necrotizing enterocolitis, mechanical ventilation, and systemic infection in the first 48 hours of life.

749
01:46:57,152 --> 01:47:04,989
No obvious benefits were gained for chronic lung disease, death in childhood, or neurodevelopmental delay in childhood.

750
01:47:04,989 --> 01:47:08,989
Therapy was not associated with chorioamnionitis.

751
01:47:08,989 --> 01:47:19,789
Parenthetically, corticosteroids given prophylactically to women at risk of preterm birth in low- and middle-income countries actually increased perinatal mortality rates .

752
01:47:19,789 --> 01:47:33,702
Because of this unexpected finding, a multicountry randomized trial comparing dexamethasone to placebo and involving pregnant women between 260/7 and 336/7 weeks’ gestation was recently completed .

753
01:47:33,702 --> 01:47:38,514
The trial was stopped early due to benefits of dexamethasone, which

754
01:47:38,668 --> 01:47:43,605
resulted in a significantly lower risk of stillbirth or neonatal death.

755
01:47:43,555 --> 01:47:50,080
The discrepancy between the two reports was attributed to inaccuracies in gestational age assessment .

756
01:47:50,080 --> 01:48:04,768
A single course of corticosteroids is currently recommended for women between 24 and 34 weeks’ gestation who are at risk for delivery within 7 days (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a).

757
01:48:04,768 --> 01:48:09,030
This recommendation for premature twins has been challenged .

758
01:48:09,030 --> 01:48:19,155
For pregnancies at 23 weeks and at risk of delivery within 7 days, a single course of corticosteroids may be considered (p. 785).

759
01:48:19,155 --> 01:48:28,793
Administration of corticosteroids during the periviable period is linked to parental decisions regarding resuscitation and should be considered in that context .

760
01:48:28,793 --> 01:48:37,218
Agent Selection Betamethasone and dexamethasone are glucocorticoids that appear equivalent in stimulating fetal lung maturation .

761
01:48:37,218 --> 01:48:51,405
Reduced rates of major preterm neonatal morbidities and increased rates of survival without neurosensory disability at age 2 years do not differ between them (Crowther, 2019; Elimian, 2007).

762
01:48:51,405 --> 01:49:00,143
A treatment course may be two 12-mg doses of betamethasone, and each dose is given intramuscularly 24 hours apart.

763
01:49:00,143 --> 01:49:07,468
With dexamethasone, 6-mg doses are given intramuscularly every 12 hours for four doses.

764
01:49:07,468 --> 01:49:22,280
Because treatment for less than 24 hours may be beneficial and reduce neonatal morbidity and mortality rates, a first dose of antenatal corticosteroids is administered regardless of the ability to complete additional doses before delivery .

765
01:49:22,280 --> 01:49:31,205
Late-preterm Delivery Antenatal betamethasone has been compared against placebo for neonates that are likely to deliver in the late-preterm period .

766
01:49:31,205 --> 01:49:48,530
Although only 60 percent of 2831 women received both injections, the rate of respiratory complications measured as a composite outcome was significantly lower with corticosteroid use compared with placebo—11.6 versus 14.4 percent.

767
01:49:48,530 --> 01:50:07,980
Because of these findings, consideration for administration of a single course of betamethasone for women between 340/7 and 366/7 weeks is advocated by both the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2016a).

768
01:50:07,980 --> 01:50:19,293
Adoption of this practice has not been universal because of both shortand long-term neonatal safety concerns (Crowther, 2016; Kamath-Rayne, 2016).

769
01:50:19,293 --> 01:50:26,318
Specifically, in the newborns receiving betamethasone, rates of hypoglycemia were significantly greater .

770
01:50:26,318 --> 01:50:34,643
Neonatal hypoglycemia is particularly worrisome for possible adverse longterm consequences that include developmental delay .

771
01:50:34,643 --> 01:50:46,605
Another caveat is that the largest effects of betamethasone included a reduction in transient tachypnea of the newborn (TTN), which is a selflimited condition with little clinical significance .

772
01:50:46,605 --> 01:50:55,368
Specifically, the rates of TTN were 6.7 and 9.9 percent in those given betamethasone and placebo, respectively.

773
01:50:55,368 --> 01:51:02,493
These rates are three- to fourfold higher than those reported by the Consortium on Safe Labor (2010).

774
01:51:02,493 --> 01:51:13,105
The latter was a retrospective, observational study from 19 hospitals across the United States that included 233,844 deliveries.

775
01:51:13,105 --> 01:51:20,593
Because of these issues, we do not provide corticosteroids beyond 34 weeks at Parkland Hospital at this time.

776
01:51:20,593 --> 01:51:30,318
Repeated Courses A single dose of intramuscular corticosteroids has been compared with repeated courses for lung maturation in two major randomized trials.

777
01:51:30,318 --> 01:51:38,580
Both found that repeated courses reduced neonatal respiratory morbidity rates, but the long-term consequences were much different.

778
01:51:38,580 --> 01:51:43,530
In one study, all women were given a primary course of betamethasone.

779
01:51:43,530 --> 01:51:50,793
If the preterm delivery risk persisted, they were assigned to serial weekly doses of betamethasone or placebo.

780
01:51:50,793 --> 01:51:56,305
These investigators found no adverse effects in the infants followed to age 2 years .

781
01:51:56,305 --> 01:52:07,630
In the second study, 495 women were randomly assigned to receive a single corticosteroid course that contained two doses or assigned to repeated courses that were given weekly .

782
01:52:07,630 --> 01:52:14,993
In infants exposed to repeated courses, a nonsignificant rise in the cerebral palsy rate was identified.

783
01:52:14,993 --> 01:52:19,618
Still, exposure to the doubled betamethasone dose was worrisome

784
01:52:19,780 --> 01:52:25,567
because some experimental evidence supports the view that adverse effects are dose dependent .

785
01:52:25,517 --> 01:52:33,030
We agree with Stiles (2007) who summarized these two studies as “early gain, long-term questions.

786
01:52:33,030 --> 01:52:43,680
At Parkland Hospital, our practice is to follow the recommendations of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) for singlecourse therapy.

787
01:52:43,680 --> 01:52:53,917
Rescue Therapy This refers to administration of a second corticosteroid dose when delivery becomes imminent and more than 7 days have elapsed since the initial dose.

788
01:52:53,917 --> 01:53:02,205
In one randomized trial, 326 women received placebo or a single 12-mg dose of betamethasone .

789
01:53:02,205 --> 01:53:07,842
Paradoxically, the rescue dose of betamethasone increased the risk of RDS.

790
01:53:07,842 --> 01:53:25,280
In another randomized study of 437 women with gestations <33 weeks, Garite and associates (2009) reported significantly lower rates of respiratory complications and neonatal composite morbidity with rescue corticosteroids versus placebo.

791
01:53:25,280 --> 01:53:30,717
Rates of perinatal mortality and other morbidities, however, did not differ.

792
01:53:30,717 --> 01:53:35,780
Another study found that treated infants had improved respiratory compliance .

793
01:53:35,780 --> 01:53:51,080
In a metaanalysis, Crowther and colleagues (2011) concluded that a single rescue course of corticosteroids should be considered in women whose prior course was administered at least 7 days previously and who were <34 weeks’ gestation.

794
01:53:51,080 --> 01:54:07,892
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) notes that a single rescue course of antenatal corticosteroids should be considered in women with fetuses <34 weeks’ gestation whose prior course was administered at least 7 days previously.

795
01:54:07,892 --> 01:54:12,417
Effects of rescue therapy beyond 34 weeks are unknown.

796
01:54:12,417 --> 01:54:20,542
At Parkland Hospital, we currently do not provide additional courses of corticosteroids beyond the initial single-course therapy.

797
01:54:20,542 --> 01:54:24,342
# Magnesium Sulfate for Neuroprotection.

798
01:54:24,342 --> 01:54:39,080
Very-low-birthweight neonates whose mothers were treated with magnesium sulfate for preterm labor or preeclampsia were found to have a reduced incidence of cerebral palsy at 3 years (Grether, 2000; Nelson, 1995).

799
01:54:39,080 --> 01:54:44,680
Because of this, randomized trials were designed to investigate this hypothesis.

800
01:54:44,680 --> 01:54:53,717
In one trial, 1063 women at risk of delivery before 30 weeks’ gestation were given magnesium sulfate or placebo .

801
01:54:53,717 --> 01:54:57,917
Magnesium exposure improved some perinatal outcomes.

802
01:54:57,917 --> 01:55:04,630
Namely, rates of both neonatal death and cerebral palsy were lower in the magnesium-treated group.

803
01:55:04,630 --> 01:55:08,430
However, this study was not sufficiently powered.

804
01:55:08,430 --> 01:55:15,280
The multicenter French trial reported by Marret and associates (2008) had similar problems.

805
01:55:15,280 --> 01:55:26,867
More convincing evidence for magnesium neuroprotection came from the randomized MFMU Network study—Beneficial Effects of Antenatal Magnesium Sulfate—BEAM—study .

806
01:55:26,867 --> 01:55:38,330
In the study, 2241 women at imminent risk for preterm birth between 24 and 31 weeks’ gestation were assigned to magnesium sulfate or placebo.

807
01:55:38,330 --> 01:55:57,567
Magnesium sulfate was given as a 6-g bolus over 20 to 30 minutes and was followed with a maintenance infusion of 2 g/hr. Magnesium sulfate was actually infusing at the time of delivery in approximately half of the treated women. Infusion continuation protocols vary by institution, including ours.

808
01:55:57,567 --> 01:56:06,530
In the BEAM study, after 12 hours with no signs of imminent delivery, the infusion was stopped and resumed if delivery again seemed imminent.

809
01:56:06,530 --> 01:56:12,680
If >6 hours had passed since discontinuation, another loading dose was given.

810
01:56:12,680 --> 01:56:18,242
A 2-year assessment was available for 96 percent of the children in the BEAM trial .

811
01:56:18,242 --> 01:56:23,967
The results can be interpreted differently depending on statistical methodologies employed.

812
01:56:23,967 --> 01:56:33,430
Some interpret these findings to mean that magnesium sulfate infusion prevents cerebral palsy regardless of the gestational age at which therapy is given.

813
01:56:33,430 --> 01:56:43,242
Those with a differing view conclude that this trial only supports use of magnesium sulfate for prevention of cerebral palsy potentially before 28 weeks.

814
01:56:43,242 --> 01:56:45,842
Table 45-10.

815
01:56:45,842 --> 01:56:57,467
Magnesium Sulfate for the Prevention of Cerebral Palsya Subsequently, Doyle and associates (2009) reviewed five randomized trials to assess neuroprotective effects.

816
01:56:57,467 --> 01:57:04,167
A total of 6145 infants were studied, and these reviewers concluded that

817
01:57:04,324 --> 01:57:11,349
magnesium sulfate exposure compared with no exposure significantly lowered risks for cerebral palsy.

818
01:57:11,299 --> 01:57:15,861
Rates of other neonatal morbidities did not differ significantly.

819
01:57:15,861 --> 01:57:22,186
It was calculated that treatment given to 63 women would prevent one case of cerebral palsy.

820
01:57:22,186 --> 01:57:32,599
Controversy surrounding magnesium sulfate efficacy for neuroprotection prompted a debate at the 2011 annual meeting of the Society for MaternalFetal Medicine.

821
01:57:32,599 --> 01:57:47,836
Rouse (2011) spoke for the benefits of magnesium sulfate, whereas Sibai (2011) challenged that the reported benefits were falsely positive due to random statistical error in the metaanalysis by Doyle (2009).

822
01:57:47,836 --> 01:57:52,686
Another peculiarity is the apparent lack of dose-response for efficacy .

823
01:57:52,686 --> 01:58:11,474
Because none of the individual studies found a benefit from magnesium sulfate for fetal neuroprotection, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020d,g) concluded that those electing prophylaxis should develop specific guidelines, and this includes those with PPROM.

824
01:58:11,474 --> 01:58:29,211
In a recent prospective observational study of infants born at 220/7 to 266/7 weeks’ gestation, exposure to both corticosteroids and magnesium sulfate was associated with lower rates of neurodevelopmental impairment and death compared with corticosteroids alone .

825
01:58:29,211 --> 01:58:36,899
At Parkland Hospital, for the periviable fetus, we individualize management as shown in Table 454.

826
01:58:36,899 --> 01:58:39,911
# Antimicrobial Treatment.

827
01:58:39,911 --> 01:58:45,686
Results have been disappointing in studies of antibiotics given to arrest preterm labor.

828
01:58:45,686 --> 01:58:57,624
From one Cochrane metaanalysis, antimicrobial prophylaxis given to women with intact membranes did not reduce preterm birth rates or affect other clinically important short-term outcomes .

829
01:58:57,624 --> 01:59:04,511
However, rates of short- and longer-term harm were higher for children of mothers exposed to antibiotics.

830
01:59:04,511 --> 01:59:19,386
In the ORACLE Collaborative Group study of 6295 women with spontaneous preterm labor, intact membranes, but without evidence of infection, women were randomly assigned to receive antimicrobial or placebo therapy .

831
01:59:19,386 --> 01:59:27,761
The primary outcomes of neonatal death, chronic lung disease, and major cerebral abnormality were similar in both groups.

832
01:59:27,761 --> 01:59:40,736
In a follow-up of the ORACLE II trial, fetal exposure to antimicrobials in this setting was associated with an increased cerebral palsy rate at age 7 years compared with that in children without fetal exposure .

833
01:59:40,736 --> 01:59:55,811
Moreover, although preterm birth is associated with bacterial vaginosis, treatment with clindamycin in more recent randomized trials has failed to lower preterm birth rates (Bellad, 2018; Subtil, 2018).

834
01:59:55,811 --> 02:00:02,836
Importantly, antimicrobial use described here is distinct from that given for GBS prophylaxis .

835
02:00:02,836 --> 02:00:05,086
# Bed Rest.

836
02:00:05,086 --> 02:00:11,574
This has been one of the most often prescribed interventions, yet one of the least supported by evidence .

837
02:00:11,574 --> 02:00:19,374
An earlier systematic review concluded that evidence neither supported nor refuted bed rest for preterm birth prevention .

838
02:00:19,374 --> 02:00:29,336
Moreover, possible harms are thromboembolic events, deconditioning, and bone loss (Kovacevich, 2000; Promislow, 2004).

839
02:00:29,336 --> 02:00:40,536
Grobman and associates (2013) reported that women with activity restriction were nearly 2.5 times more likely to have a preterm birth before 34 weeks.

840
02:00:40,536 --> 02:00:44,949
This finding, however, may reflect ascertainment bias.

841
02:00:44,949 --> 02:00:53,224
That is, women with restricted activity may have been assigned to bed rest because they were viewed to have a greater risk for preterm delivery.

842
02:00:53,224 --> 02:01:06,724
The Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020a) and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020f) recommend against activity restriction to reduce preterm birth rates.

843
02:01:06,724 --> 02:01:09,411
# Cervical Pessaries.

844
02:01:09,411 --> 02:01:17,636
Silicone rings, such as the Arabin pessary, have been used to support the cervix in women with a sonographically short cervix.

845
02:01:17,636 --> 02:01:28,236
In one study, 385 women with a cervical length ≤25 mm were provided a silicone pessary or expectant management .

846
02:01:28,236 --> 02:01:38,749
Newborns spontaneously delivered before 34 weeks’ gestation in 6 percent of women in the pessary group compared with 27 percent in the expectant management group.

847
02:01:38,749 --> 02:01:50,711
In another randomized trial, almost 100 women with a cervical length <25 mm at 20 to 24 weeks’ gestation received either pessaries or expectant management

848
02:01:50,860 --> 02:01:55,622
The pessary did not lower the rate of delivery <34 weeks.

849
02:01:55,572 --> 02:01:58,535
Others have reported similar findings .

850
02:01:58,535 --> 02:02:19,722
Moreover, authors of a recent systematic review totaling 4687 women and 7167 infants found that current evidence does not support pessary use to prevent preterm birth or improve perinatal outcomes in singleton or twin gestations with a short cervix or in unselected twin gestations .

851
02:02:19,722 --> 02:02:28,860
The Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2017) currently recommends pessary prophylaxis only within research protocols.

852
02:02:28,860 --> 02:02:32,235
# Emergency or Rescue Cerclage.

853
02:02:32,235 --> 02:02:40,497
Some evidence supports the concept that cervical incompetence and preterm labor lie along a spectrum leading to preterm delivery.

854
02:02:40,497 --> 02:02:47,347
Consequently, cerclage placement after preterm labor begins to manifest clinically has been studied.

855
02:02:47,347 --> 02:02:53,760
Importantly, for women with labor and active cervical change, cerclage is contraindicated.

856
02:02:53,760 --> 02:03:05,422
In one study, 23 women with cervical incompetence before 27 weeks’ gestation were randomly assigned to bed rest, with or without an adjunctive emergency McDonald cerclage .

857
02:03:05,422 --> 02:03:15,110
Delivery delay was significantly greater in the cerclage group compared with those assigned to bed rest alone—54 versus 24 days, respectively.

858
02:03:15,110 --> 02:03:23,610
Terkildsen and coworkers (2003) studied 116 women who underwent second-trimester emergency cerclage.

859
02:03:23,610 --> 02:03:36,335
Nulliparity, membranes extending beyond the external cervical os, and cerclage before 22 weeks’ gestation were associated with a significantly lower chance of significant pregnancy continuation.

860
02:03:36,335 --> 02:03:49,710
More recently, a multicenter randomized trial assessed women with twin pregnancies and asymptomatic cervical dilation of 1 to 5 cm between 160/7 and 236/7 weeks’ gestation .

861
02:03:49,710 --> 02:03:59,097
From a total of 30 enrolled women, 17 women received physical examination-indicated cerclage, and 13 women received no cerclage.

862
02:03:59,097 --> 02:04:04,960
Of note, all women who underwent cerclage received indomethacin and antibiotics.

863
02:04:04,960 --> 02:04:17,910
In the cerclage group, the preterm birth rate was significantly decreased at all evaluated gestational ages, and specifically, cerclage lowered the birth rate for those <28 weeks by 50 percent.

864
02:04:17,910 --> 02:04:29,685
From these limited reports, for women facing a poor pregnancy prognosis due to cervical dilation at midgestation, emergency or rescue cerclage with appropriate counseling is reasonable.

865
02:04:29,685 --> 02:04:38,860
At this time, however, it is unclear if such interventions truly confer a benefit or merely increase the risk of membrane rupture and infection .

866
02:04:38,860 --> 02:04:41,235
# Tocolysis.

867
02:04:41,235 --> 02:04:47,735
Although several drugs have been used to prevent or inhibit preterm labor, none is completely effective.

868
02:04:47,735 --> 02:05:00,922
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) has concluded that tocolytic agents do not markedly prolong gestation but may delay delivery in some women for up to 48 hours.

869
02:05:00,922 --> 02:05:09,422
This may allow transport to an obstetrical center with higher-level neonatal care and permit time for a course of corticosteroid therapy.

870
02:05:09,422 --> 02:05:27,347
Although delivery may be delayed to administer corticosteroids, evidence does not support that tocolytic therapy has any direct favorable effect on neonatal outcomes or that any prolongation of pregnancy afforded by tocolytics alone translates into statistically significant neonatal benefit.

871
02:05:27,347 --> 02:05:37,285
Beta-adrenergic agonists, magnesium sulfate, calcium-channel blockers, or indomethacin are the recommended tocolytic agents for short-term use.

872
02:05:37,285 --> 02:05:41,385
The gestational age range for tocolytic use is debatable .

873
02:05:41,385 --> 02:05:52,410
But, because the perinatal outcomes in preterm neonates are generally good after 34 weeks’ gestation, most do not recommend use of tocolytics after 33 weeks.

874
02:05:52,410 --> 02:05:58,972
In many women, tocolytics stop contractions temporarily but rarely prevent preterm birth.

875
02:05:58,972 --> 02:06:10,760
Moreover, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) has concluded that maintenance therapy with tocolytics is ineffective for preventing preterm birth.

876
02:06:10,760 --> 02:06:18,860
No trial has ever convincingly shown reduced rates of any important adverse outcome by a tocolytic drug compared with placebo .

877
02:06:18,860 --> 02:06:31,610
β-Adrenergic Receptor Agonists Several compounds stimulate β-adrenergic receptors to reduce intracellular ionized calcium levels and prevent activation of myometrial contractile proteins .

878
02:06:31,610 --> 02:06:33,535
Of β-mimetic

879
02:06:33,700 --> 02:06:42,825
drugs in the United States, ritodrine and terbutaline have been used in obstetrics, but only ritodrine is FDA-approved for preterm labor.

880
02:06:42,775 --> 02:06:53,225
Ritodrine was voluntarily withdrawn from the United States market in 2003, but a discussion of ritodrine is included here to present issues with βmimetic drug use.

881
02:06:53,225 --> 02:07:01,650
In a randomized trial at Parkland Hospital, intravenous ritodrine delayed delivery for 24 hours but without other benefits .

882
02:07:01,650 --> 02:07:06,412
Additional studies confirmed a delivery delay up to 48 hours .

883
02:07:06,412 --> 02:07:13,100
Importantly, β-agonist infusion resulted in serious and even fatal maternal side effects.

884
02:07:13,100 --> 02:07:18,550
Pulmonary edema is a special concern, and its cause is multifactorial.

885
02:07:18,550 --> 02:07:33,175
Risk factors include tocolytic therapy with β-agonist drugs, multifetal gestation, concurrent corticosteroid therapy, tocolysis for more than 24 hours, and intravenous infusion of large volumes of crystalloid.

886
02:07:33,175 --> 02:07:42,300
βAgonist agents cause retention of sodium and water, and with time—usually 24 to 48 hours—these can cause volume overload .

887
02:07:42,300 --> 02:07:50,525
The drugs have been implicated in increased capillary permeability, cardiac rhythm disturbances, and myocardial ischemia.

888
02:07:50,525 --> 02:08:01,762
For example, in an earlier study, tocolysis was the third most common cause of acute respiratory distress and death in pregnant women during a 14-year period in Mississippi .

889
02:08:01,762 --> 02:08:07,125
Terbutaline is commonly used in the United States to forestall preterm labor.

890
02:08:07,125 --> 02:08:10,925
Like ritodrine, it may cause pulmonary edema .

891
02:08:10,925 --> 02:08:23,637
Lowdose terbutaline can be administered long-term by subcutaneous pump, but randomized trials fail to show benefit for such therapy (Guinn, 1998; Wenstrom, 1997).

892
02:08:23,637 --> 02:08:30,687
Oral terbutaline also is ineffective (How, 1995; Parilla, 1993).

893
02:08:30,687 --> 02:08:43,775
In one trial, 203 women with arrested preterm labor at 24 to 34 weeks’ gestation were randomly assigned to receive 5-mg terbutaline tablets or placebo every 4 hours .

894
02:08:43,775 --> 02:08:55,487
Of outcomes, delivery rates at 1 week, median days of pregnancy extension, mean gestational age at delivery, and incidence of preterm labor relapse were similar in both groups.

895
02:08:55,487 --> 02:09:04,525
Because of serious maternal side effects, the FDA (2011) issued a warning regarding terbutaline use for preterm labor.

896
02:09:04,525 --> 02:09:16,912
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) recommends only short-term inpatient use of terbutaline as a tocolytic or as acute therapy for uterine tachysystole.

897
02:09:16,912 --> 02:09:23,687
Subcutaneous dosages of 0.25 mg are commonly used for the latter indication.

898
02:09:23,687 --> 02:09:31,287
Magnesium Sulfate Ionic magnesium in a sufficiently high concentration can alter myometrial contractility.

899
02:09:31,287 --> 02:09:38,287
It functions as a calcium antagonist, and when given in pharmacological doses, it may inhibit labor.

900
02:09:38,287 --> 02:09:43,275
Magnesium sulfate treatment has been associated with pulmonary edema .

901
02:09:43,275 --> 02:09:56,750
As discussed in Chapter 41 (p. 720), this has not been our experience at Parkland Hospital with treatment of tens of thousands of preeclamptic women with intramuscular or intravenous magnesium sulfate.

902
02:09:56,750 --> 02:10:04,962
In one randomized trial, 54 women with preterm labor received magnesium sulfate, ritodrine, or placebo.

903
02:10:04,962 --> 02:10:08,375
Few differences in outcomes were identified .

904
02:10:08,375 --> 02:10:17,950
Cox and coworkers (1990) randomly assigned 156 women to receive magnesium sulfate or infusions of normal saline.

905
02:10:17,950 --> 02:10:24,962
Magnesium sulfate–treated women and their neonates had identical outcomes compared with those given placebo.

906
02:10:24,962 --> 02:10:30,562
Because of these findings, this method of tocolysis was abandoned at Parkland Hospital.

907
02:10:30,562 --> 02:10:40,662
Similarly, Crowther and associates (2014) concluded that magnesium sulfate to treat preterm labor was ineffective and potentially harmful.

908
02:10:40,662 --> 02:10:54,287
Last, the FDA (2013) has warned against prolonged use of magnesium sulfate given to arrest preterm labor because of bone thinning and fractures in fetuses exposed for more than 5 to 7 days.

909
02:10:54,287 --> 02:10:58,150
This was attributed to low calcium levels in the fetus.

910
02:10:58,150 --> 02:11:06,175
Yule and associates (2020) demonstrated the influence of magnesium sulfate on maternal serum calcium levels.

911
02:11:06,175 --> 02:11:12,362
Prostaglandin Inhibitors These compounds are intimately involved in the contractions of normal labor .

912
02:11:12,362 --> 02:11:19,150
Antagonists act by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis or by blocking their action on target organs.

913
02:11:19,150 --> 02:11:25,762
Indomethacin, a nonselective cyclooxygenase inhibitor, was first used as a tocolytic in

914
02:11:25,924 --> 02:11:28,461
one study of 50 women .

915
02:11:28,411 --> 02:11:38,686
Studies that followed reported the efficacy of indomethacin in halting contractions and delaying preterm birth (Muench, 2003; Niebyl, 1980).

916
02:11:38,686 --> 02:11:52,161
Morales and coworkers (1989, 1993a), however, compared indomethacin with either ritodrine or magnesium sulfate and found no difference in their efficacy to forestall preterm delivery.

917
02:11:52,161 --> 02:12:04,099
Berghella and associates (2006) reviewed four trials of indomethacin given to women with a sonographically determined short cervix and found such therapy to be ineffective to halt labor.

918
02:12:04,099 --> 02:12:08,899
But, as discussed earlier, its use with cerclage is gaining favor.

919
02:12:08,899 --> 02:12:19,086
Most studies have limited indomethacin use to 24 to 48 hours because of concerns for oligohydramnios, which can develop with therapeutic doses.

920
02:12:19,086 --> 02:12:27,511
If amnionic fluid is monitored, oligohydramnios can be detected early, and it is reversible with drug discontinuation.

921
02:12:27,511 --> 02:12:42,324
There has also been considerable debate on the association of necrotizing enterocolitis or early ductus arteriosus closure and use of indomethacin (Doni, 2020; Hammers, 2015; Muench, 2001).

922
02:12:42,324 --> 02:12:53,249
Two metaanalyses of the effects of antenatal indomethacin on neonatal outcomes had conflicting findings (Amin, 2007; Loe, 2005).

923
02:12:53,249 --> 02:13:04,399
In one review of 20 studies, cyclooxygenase inhibitors, including indomethacin, provided no clear benefit compared with placebo or any other tocolytic agent .

924
02:13:04,399 --> 02:13:14,874
Calcium-channel Blockers Myometrial activity is directly related to cytoplasmic free calcium, and reduced calcium concentrations inhibit contractions.

925
02:13:14,874 --> 02:13:21,949
Calcium-channel blockers inhibit, by various mechanisms, calcium entry through cell membrane channels.

926
02:13:21,949 --> 02:13:35,311
From study results, calcium-channel blockers, especially nifedipine, are safer and more effective tocolytic agents than β-agonist drugs (King, 2003; Papatsonis, 1997).

927
02:13:35,311 --> 02:13:47,586
Comparing magnesium sulfate and nifedipine, one study of 192 women at 24 to 33 weeks’ gestation found no substantial differences in efficacy or adverse effects .

928
02:13:47,586 --> 02:14:00,024
Comparing nifedipine and atosiban in 145 women with preterm labor between 24 and 33 weeks, another randomized study showed that neither agent was superior to delay delivery.

929
02:14:00,024 --> 02:14:03,286
Neonatal morbidity rates were equivalent .

930
02:14:03,286 --> 02:14:14,799
Flenady and coworkers (2014b) reviewed 38 trials of calcium-channel blockers for preterm labor and concluded that these agents have benefits compared with placebo.

931
02:14:14,799 --> 02:14:28,211
It is problematic, however, that this conclusion stemmed from a trial with unclear risk of selection bias and a three-arm study of 84 women that was not blinded (Ara, 2008; Zhang, 2002).

932
02:14:28,211 --> 02:14:40,049
More recently, Hawkins and associates (2021) performed a randomized, placebo-controlled trial of nifedipine for acute tocolysis of preterm labor at Parkland Hospital.

933
02:14:40,049 --> 02:14:45,124
Acute tocolysis with nifedipine did not affect preterm birth rates.

934
02:14:45,124 --> 02:14:52,861
However, the study results were obtained following an interim analysis demonstrating futility for study continuation.

935
02:14:52,861 --> 02:15:00,036
Importantly, the combination of nifedipine and magnesium sulfate for tocolysis is potentially dangerous.

936
02:15:00,036 --> 02:15:14,074
Ben-Ami (1994) and Kurtzman (1993) and their coworkers reported that nifedipine enhances the neuromuscular blocking effects of magnesium, which can interfere with pulmonary and cardiac function.

937
02:15:14,074 --> 02:15:25,024
In one small study of 54 women with preterm labor who received either magnesium sulfate plus nifedipine or no tocolytic, neither benefit nor harm was found .

938
02:15:25,024 --> 02:15:34,799
Other Agents Atosiban is a nonapeptide oxytocin analogue and an oxytocin-receptor antagonist that is used in European countries to forestall labor.

939
02:15:34,799 --> 02:15:48,499
In randomized trials, atosiban failed to improve relevant neonatal outcomes and was linked with significant neonatal morbidity (Flenady, 2014a; Moutquin, 2000; Romero, 2000).

940
02:15:48,499 --> 02:15:54,799
The FDA has denied approval of atosiban for use in the United States because of these findings.

941
02:15:54,799 --> 02:16:02,024
Nitric oxide donors are potent smooth-muscle relaxants that affect the vasculature, gut, and uterus.

942
02:16:02,024 --> 02:16:13,524
In randomized clinical trials, nitroglycerin administered orally, transdermally, or intravenously was ineffective or showed no superiority compared with other tocolytics.

943
02:16:13,524 --> 02:16:21,486
In addition, maternal hypotension is a common side effect (Bisits, 2004; Lees, 1999).

944
02:16:21,486 --> 02:16:23,474
# Labor

945
02:16:23,644 --> 02:16:25,369
and Delivery.

946
02:16:25,319 --> 02:16:30,269
Fetal heart rate and uterine contractions are monitored with preterm labor.

947
02:16:30,269 --> 02:16:33,769
We prefer continuous electronic monitoring.

948
02:16:33,769 --> 02:16:39,369
Fetal tachycardia, especially with ruptured membranes, suggests sepsis.

949
02:16:39,369 --> 02:16:48,369
Some evidence supports that intrapartum acidemia may intensify some of the neonatal complications usually attributed to preterm delivery.

950
02:16:48,369 --> 02:17:01,419
For example, Morgan and associates (2017) found that metabolic acidemia significantly raised the risks related to prematurity in neonates delivered <34 weeks’ gestation.

951
02:17:01,419 --> 02:17:13,819
Low and colleagues (1995) observed that intrapartum acidosis—umbilical artery blood pH <7.0 —had an important role in neonatal complications.

952
02:17:13,819 --> 02:17:21,731
GBS infections are common and dangerous in the preterm neonate, and antimicrobial prophylaxis should be provided .

953
02:17:21,731 --> 02:17:30,831
For delivery, perinatal outcome data do not support routine episiotomy or forceps delivery to protect the “fragile” preterm fetal head.

954
02:17:30,831 --> 02:17:40,131
Staff proficient in resuscitative techniques commensurate with the gestational age and fully oriented to any specific problems should be present at delivery.

955
02:17:40,131 --> 02:17:50,769
The importance of specialized personnel and facilities for preterm newborn care is underscored by the improved survival rates of these neonates when delivered in tertiary-care centers.

956
02:17:50,769 --> 02:17:58,669
For community-based hospitals, multidisciplinary team-based simulation for preterm delivery can improve performance .

957
02:17:58,669 --> 02:18:06,719
Preterm newborns frequently have intracranial germinal matrix bleeding that can extend to more serious intraventricular hemorrhage.

958
02:18:06,719 --> 02:18:14,794
It was hypothesized that cesarean delivery to obviate trauma from labor and vaginal delivery might prevent these complications.

959
02:18:14,794 --> 02:18:18,444
This has not been validated by subsequent studies .

960
02:18:18,444 --> 02:18:24,769
At Parkland Hospital, cesarean delivery is reserved for usual obstetrical indications.

